Inflation in Europe has jumped because of food
Accelerating inflation measured in the euro area is mainly due to a steady rise in food prices.
November's price growth in 13 eurozone countries was above expectations higher, after 2.6 per cent in October, annual inflation of 3.1 percent was registered, the Luxembourg-based Union statistics office announced.
The official forecast projected only 3 percent, Bloomberg said.
Unlike the U.S., British and Canadian issuance banks, the European Central Bank (ECB) has not lowered the basic interest rate, saying the reduction, along with rising raw material prices and falling unemployment, would lead to an inflation spiral.
The ECB would like to keep the rate of inflation below two percent, but in their vicinity.
According to one of the analysts, the ECB is in an absurd situation: it has to "deter inflation" so that it doesn't have to do anything on this issue later.
In Germany, November's inflation of 3.3 percent represents a 15-year record; in Spain, it rose from 3.6 to 4.1 percent.
Eurozone inflation has escalated due to sharply rising food and energy prices.
Wheat prices have risen by 88 percent, also the soybean exchange rate reached its highest value since 1973.
Likewise, it was necessary in 2007 to pay significantly more for edible oil and dairy products.
The price of oil on world markets rose 52 percent this year, and last month, one barrel of black gold was already worth nearly $100.
According to the ECB, inflation will jump from 2.1 percent this year to 2.5 this year, but it will already fall to 1.9 in 2009.
According to analysts, the level of 12-month inflation in the next 3-4 months will continue to be around 3 percent, but its average annual rate will be 2.1 percent.
At the same time, they expect the ECB to cut interest rates twice in 2008.
Gallup sees government crisis
After prolonged stagnation at the beginning of December, Fidesz’s circle grew significantly, and now has the largest camp since 2002, while in the case of the Socialists the Gallup Institute has never measured so low – 13 percent support.
In the case of certain voters of the party, the difference - due to the greater determination of opposition voters - would represent more than two-thirds (71 percent) support over the five-fifth MSZP support.
According to the survey, MDF and SZDSZ are below the threshold for entering parliament: their support within the entire population is two percent.
Among certain party voters, supporters of both camps are one percent more.
The opinion of the public on the economy has also deteriorated due to the previous minimum - there has never been the ratio of those who consider the economic situation of the country to be very bad, so high - 41 percent, while, according to another 46 percent, the situation is quite bad.
Families evaluate the change of their material situation after spring and summer more hopeful prospects now more pessimistically.
As for next year, a larger part of the population expects further deterioration and only a tenth with improvement.
In December, 71 percent of respondents judged, most of any Gallup Institute poll so far that the prime minister is doing his job badly rather than well.
Ferenci Gyurcsány is believed or highly trusted by 13 percent of respondents, while for Viktor Orbán the proportion of supporters is 38 percent.
54 percent don't trust the prime minister at all, the chairman of the Fidesz movement 27 percent.
One of the indicators of the development of the political situation is how much of the public believes that dissatisfaction with the prime minister can be addressed without changing the government.
While after last October's riots, 35 percent of the government and prime minister were considered desirable, at present this share is already only 20 percent, with a relative majority (33 percent) seeing the solution only in calling a new election.
It is symptomatic that 60 percent of the respondents responded to a question about their eventual participation, that it would definitely come to the referendum and another 20 percent said that it would probably come.
By comparison, "parliamentary elections held this Sunday" would certainly be attended by only 55 percent, and another 15 percent consider their turnout likely. The success of the referendum would be undoubted on the basis of current intentions.
The tuitioner would be told not 74, doctor's fees 79 and fees for hospital stays of 84 percent.
If we look at the same shares only within the circle of people certainly promising their participation, then the introduction of tuition fees, doctor's fees and hospital stay fees would be rejected by 81, 84 and 89 percent, respectively.
The most expensive is Auchan, nearly one-fifthos more expensive by Tesco
Hypermarkets in the Budaörs business triangle have maintained last year's, on average 16-percent increase in the price level, with the difference that there has been no increase in VAT this year.
Napi Gazdaság
Napi Gazdaság’s usual Christmas consumer basket in Budaör’s shopping triangle has increased by an average of 16 percent this year, but in all hypermarkets, more than 1,000 forints have risen in prices.
As prices in supermarkets "flowed out" as early as last December, the percentage did not show such a drastic increase due to an earlier high basis.
However, it is certainly gloomy for consumers that what they could easily buy last year at a price below nine thousand forints will not now be bought even for ten thousand.
The consumer basket of the Daily Economy in examined hypermarkets exceeded the psychological limit of ten thousand forints by at least 91 forints.
The highest price of the basket was 10 573 forints.
(In 2003, the consumer basket cost 6,800 forints.)
The price of Unico and living fish remained almost at last year's level, but very expensive cruel breasts, nuts and poppies.
Breach and flour prices corresponded to data from the Central Statistical Office, although in Auchan we also founded kilogram bread for 139 forints.
Competition did not go with the price of the product below 200 forints.
Likewise, it was surprising in this store that the price of a two-litre Coca Cola in a returnable bottle was below 200 forints, so even with a deposit, the product cost only 257 forints.
Even so, according to Napi Gazdaság Auchan's consumer basket this year, it appeared to be the most expensive, while Cora was the cheapest.
According to our calculations, prices featured in the consumer basket of the Napi Gazdaság, due to prices last Christmas Cora by 15, while both Tesco and Auchan by about 18 per cent.
When comparing last year's highest and this year's lowest values of the consumer basket, the difference was 12 percent, while the increase between last year's lowest and this year's highest is 21 percent.
In forints, there was an increment of an average of 1 480.
The average price increase last year came to 1 206 forints, with prices affected by VAT rises last Christmas.
It is precisely this process that has disrupted the trade, the department stores have clearly not been able to decide how large the price margins for individual products to determine, since they have failed to predict the impact of a change in prices on consumption.
(If the trader plans the rate of circulation of the product poorly, it may lose a significant profit due to the price range adapted to the intensity of circulation.)
Due to VAT, the rise in the price of Napi Gazdaság’s consumer basket proved so extreme last year that the price of the most expensive basket was 1,720 forints higher than in 2006, while for the cheapest, the price difference was 844 forints.
This year there was no extreme difference, the most expensive basket now cost 1 630 forints more than last year, and the cheapest also increased by 1,284 forints.
Comparing to both Christmas seasons, a catastrophic increase in prices came to light, in less than 20 months the goods of daily consumption in hypermarkets increased by more than 30 percent.
(This, among other things, is not entirely confirmed by the data of the Central Statistical Office: in the case of several basic foods, it has measured a price increase exceeding 30 percent within 12 months.
In addition to flour, for example, in the case of eggs.)
In the latter case, however, according to the consumer basket of the Daily Economy, this seems almost impossible, because the price level of the product, quite expensive already last year, showed a price growth of little above 10 percent, given the basis even in the worst case.
Flour suppliers seem to occupy stronger positions than egg suppliers, they have also managed to achieve a drastic increase in prices in international chains.
Hypermarkets in a commercial gold triangle don't complain, they're still reporting on a good year.
According to our experience, the majority of customers in the three visited stores are no longer interested in prices.
They buy a lot of things, they take durable food by salaries.
On Wednesday morning there were long queues at the ticket offices in all three supermarkets, as the number of visitors increased sharply.
However, as we know, in the countryside the turnover is lower, several international chains complain to the research companies that sales also lag behind their plans, not only in the case of food, but also in durable consumer goods.
In many places it is saved on disinfectants
This year, more than 1,000 cases of infection have been registered in hospitals primarily due to inappropriate use of antibiotics.
Hygienic conditions of hospitals, overcrowding of beds, overburdening of staff stopping the infection do not help, according to the head of the National Epidemiological Center.
In many places it is also saved on disinfectants.
She was recently diagnosed with MRSA infection at St. John's Hospital Traumatology Department.
Antibiotic-resistant bacteria have been discovered in a man who arrived at the hospital after a serious traffic accident.
Patient has been isolated.
A similar infection occurred in the same place at the beginning of the year.
The patient injured in a serious car accident was discharged home, but not long after he had to return with MRSA infection, and due to the bacteria located in his body, one important operation could not yet be performed.
By December 10 this year, 1 094 hospital infections caused by multi-drug-resistant disease agents had been reported to the Nesocomialis Surveillance National System, Karolina Böröcz, head of the National Epidemiological Centre, said.
This means that so many infections caused by multi-drug agents of the disease have been certainly registered in hospitals, others are unknown.
Nearly two-thirds (683) of infections were caused by MRSA, or methicillin-oxacillin-resistant golden staphylococcus.
Staphylococcus aureus (SA) is worn by a third of people in the nose without causing the disease.
Infections caused by SA (e.g. cervical inflammation, inflammation of the tonsils, skin infections, early infections) respond well to antibiotics and can be treated well.
However, when antibiotics are given to an exaggerated or inappropriate extent, it develops from a bacterium that responds well to antibiotic bacteria that is very resistant to antibiotics and that spreads rapidly in inadequate hospital conditions.
Especially in the case of temporary or permanently poor functioning of the immune system in patients with a weakened organism, low body weight or in patients in years, it can cause serious hospital infections, such as early infection or blood poisoning - sepsis.
According to St John's chief medical officer, there is nothing special about MRSA infection, similar infections belong in hospitals for daily practice.
"Infection must not be confused with an epidemic, MRSA is not just there," Tibor Nyulasi declared, "where the presence of the agent of the disease is not investigated."
Resists antibiotics
"We insulate these patients, the attending staff treat them in rubber gloves and in masks, and we have the necessary antibiotics to treat them," Tibor Nyulasi said.
Patients admitted to the hospital are first examined to see if they are present in their organism MRSA, but the infection cannot always be avoided.
Sampling is not in vogue
The vast majority of MRSA hospital infections could be prevented, but the current conditions and conditions in domestic hospitals are not favourable for the prevention and spread of multidrug-resistant diseases, according to Karolina Böröcz.
Since this infection occurs symptomatically in hospitals and homes for the elderly, hospitals receiving patients should insulate the MRSA carriers immediately.
However, microbiological sampling is not fashionable in Hungarian hospitals.
It also plays a role in the fact that the National Health Insurance Fund does not finance these examinations.
Most cases are thus uncovered if the patient suffers from an observable MRSA infection, the epidemiological center's chief of staff said.
The European Union has strict recommendations for appropriate treatment with antibiotics in human medicine, and there are also domestic directives for the treatment of individual infections, medical records.
Microbiological examinations should be carried out in order to prevent the development of resistance to drugs, but hospitals do not comply with this in all cases.
Samples of microbiological samples are even rarer for basic or outpatient treatment of patients, Karolina Böröczová said.
Saves on disinfectants
According to scientific evidence, MRSA spreads among patients most often through medical staff.
Therefore, the washing of hands and the use of gloves and masks plays a very important role, but there are also serious deficiencies in hospitals.
MRSA is transmitted by droplet infection even by touch, but it is not always possible to insulate infected patients under the conditions of domestic hospitals.
Rooms, on the other hand, are often overcrowded, beds are often very close together, although from an epidemiological point of view, patients should be at least 1.5 meters away.
As Karolina Böröcz noted, unfortunately, we can also find that in hospitals that find themselves in an unfavorable material situation, they save on disinfectants, caregivers are few and patients a lot, which increases the chances of developing infections.
According to Karolina Böröcz, where the necessary protective measures are observed, similar infections are demonstrably less or the further spread of the agent of the disease can be stopped.
The government's anti-MRSA programme
Treating MRSA infection is a huge expense for health insurers, which is which is being combated.
For example, in England, the government has written a programme to prevent MRSA, respectively, to reduce its incidence.
It seems that the programme is successful, according to published data, MRSA infections in England show a downward trend.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) ranks among its priorities for the prevention of the development of multi-resistant disease agents (including MRSA) and hospital infections caused by these disease agents.
The police captain who caused the fatal accident was not drunk
An official expert's assessment in the case of police captain of the XXII district who caused a fatal accident is complete.
It can be ruled out that the police captain of the XXII district, who killed a pedestrian on Tuesday and who in the meantime resigned from his post, was driving under the influence of alcohol or intoxicants, a spokesman for the High Public Prosecutor’s Office of the capital Attila Morvai reported on Friday after the expert opinion of the case was completed.
The expert's opinion confirmed the outcome of the initial investigation, which has already stated that the former police captain did no alcohol before sitting behind the wheel.
The then director of the police headquarters of the XXII. district collided on Tuesday in Lajose Kossutha street in the XXII district by his own passenger car at the turn of a fifty-five-year-old man who entered in front of him while crossing the roadway outside the marked crossing.
(The latter fact that a pedestrian passed outside the zebra, after all, according to the rules of the road is not a mitigating circumstance, the turning car must still give preference.)
After being transported to the hospital, the pedestrian died.
Due to these events, the district police director resigned from his post and asked to be retired, according to the statement of the police, because he was so shaken by the case.
The Budapest Investigative Public Prosecutor’s Office conducts an investigation into the matter.
Attila Morvai also mentioned that the former police captain had cooperated with prosecutors conducting investigations from the beginning.
Another 400 million euros for infrastructure
An agreement on three loan frameworks totaling 400 million euros to support education, transport and environmental infrastructure as well as energy was signed by Finance Minister János Veres and Marta Gajecka, Vice-President of the European Investment Bank (EIB), in Budapest on Friday.
The European Investment Bank (EIB) approved a framework worth 300 million euros in 2006 for the Hungarian budgetary financing of transport and environmental projects carried out by the Cohesion Fund, while the parties had previously concluded a loan agreement of 100 million, the now-signed contract concerned the use of the remaining 200 million euros.
A loan agreement of 150 million euros is also used for the budgetary financing of projects related to education.
The EIB has approved a total of 300 million euros for Hungary for these purposes, the use of the remaining 150 million euros can take place later, with the extension of the credit agreement.
In the field of innovation, the parties signed a contract for 50 million euros, which will use the budget primarily for the Debrecen University research-development project.
There was also talking about the 4th metro line at the meeting.
The parties agreed that after Hungary agrees with the EU details of metro funding, it will return to whether and how the original treaty needs to be modified.
According to János Veres, this could happen in the first quarter of 2008.
The EIB has financed a total of 113 projects in Hungary since 1990.
The interest on loans granted by the EIB with an eight-year waiting period and without an obligation commission shall be linked to the EURIBOR rate.
Bulgaria is an EU real estate paradise
The development of Bulgaria's real estate market is staggering - the attention of the whole of Europe is turning to a previously slightly neglected Balkan country.
What, where and who buys on the still cheapest, but fast-paced EU market?
Analysis of Re/Max's Bulgarian Division.
The most sought-after Eastern European destination for real estate purchases is no doubt Bulgaria with its cheap ski apartments and houses on the sea coast.
Of the mountains are mostly in the course areas Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitoša, on the sea coast Albena, Golden Sands, Sunny Beach, the ancient towns of Sozopol and Nessebar, but more and more people are also looking for properties around Kavarna and Balčik.
According to analysts from Re/Max Bulgaria, sooner or later the interest of investors and the central part of the country - for example Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, Triavna - and more and more buyers are also shopping in the vicinity of the downright cheap city of Ruse.
Also, the capital of Sofia is a promise of tempting possibilities, business and office premises promise the highest return.
Bulgaria is the best way to turn into one of Europe's most appealing golf paradises and thus become, in addition to the summer and winter season, a popular destination for trips in the other two seasons.
Anticipatory investors are already buying in bulk, for example around Bansko.
According to analysts, property buyers in Bulgaria are also trying to change their lives and lifestyles.
Most customers come from England, but there are also buyers from France, Belgium, Spain and, more and more and more often from Romania.
Maltese and Cypriot investors are also present here, there are traditionally many Russian customers by the Black Sea and in ski paradises.
Foreign buyers can be divided into three groups, says Georgiy Jankov, regional director of Re/Max Bulgaria.
Settling people buy either renovated properties worth 50-100 thousand euros, or dilapidated old houses for 5-15 thousand euros, which are then renovated.
They require good accessibility and possibilities of communication, the proximity of a large city, shops and, as a rule, a local doctor is important for them.
The second category is cottage buyers (Bulgaria was ranked among the top 10 German holiday destinations in December).
The third group consists of commercial investors who buy in hopes of an increase in value in the medium- or long-term.
The increase in real estate in the capital is continuous, as demand is getting stronger.
In Bulgaria, however, real estate prices are the lowest in the European Union, they have grown by 21 percent annually in the last five years.
Increasing property prices significantly exceeds pay rises.
Fodor and Hungarian six percent
According to environmental and water minister GÁbor Fodor, a meeting of experts will be concluded on Tuesday, and after Wednesday's official opening, an international conference on the climate regime after 2012 will continue with a meeting of ministers.
Experts and politicians from 192 countries are discussing the Bali Roadmap, which addresses the efforts following the period of emission reduction as defined by the Kyoto Protocol until 2012.
At the moment, there is a tumultuous debate about them between developing and developed countries.
Also, the United States announced that it now does not wish to negotiate accurate numbers, although it is leaning towards an agreement to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 25-40 percent by 2020, relative to the state in 1990.
Another big question of the conference is whether, in addition to reducing emissions, the developed countries will also proceed to provide both material and technological assistance to countries that are dependent on it.
The agreement was also born on subsidies for developing countries.
The subsidy fund established by the Kyoto Protocol will be led by a 16-member management board, most likely under the World Environmental Protection Programme (GEF) at the United Nations - we can read in the journal Piac és Profit.
Hungary's six percent
On behalf of Hungary, Environment and Water Minister GÁbor Fodor supports the EU's efforts to maintain a leading role in efforts for global climate protection and to help establish a new international agreement by 2009.
The minister said the EU pledges to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 20 percent by 2020 compared to 1990 levels, independently of the success of adopting a "post-2012 agreement", and is ready to commit to up to a 30 percent reduction if other developed regions of the world, respectively developed countries, are willing to take responsibility and commitments appropriate to their capabilities.
Gábor Fodor pointed out that the countries that joined the Kyoto Protocol in 1997 set themselves the target of a reduction of 5.2 percent, with the then 15 EU member states committing to 8 percent.
Hungary’s commitment is to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 6 percent compared to 1985-87, which will be fulfilled by our homeland. - is recalled in a press release from the Ministry of the Environment.
There is a lot going on in Bali, because within two years it is necessary to adopt an international agreement on a long-term policy of climate change, otherwise its entry into force would certainly be postponed until 2012. In this case, a new emission reduction system would not immediately build on the commitments set by the Kyoto Protocol, the necessity of which, in its latest report, was also highlighted by the international scientific corps on climate change - the IPCC, which is the recipient of the Nobel Peace.
This would lead to a delay in reducing emissions and an unavoidable increase in the average temperature on Earth by more than 2 degrees Celsius, which, according to scientists, would have extremely serious and irreversible consequences.
The British military is conducting recruitment on YouTube
U.K. military units arrate on a video-sharing servior for recordings of actions in Iraq and Afghanistan making themselves popular.
British Air Forces (RAF): Special Forces operating in Afghanistan have been uploading videos of anti-Talian actions since March 2006, the BBC reported.
The recordings, taken by a miniature camera attached to a helmet, are controlled by Air Force Air Force's command operations in the Kandahar region and then sent further to London, where they are transmitted to the Internet.
The political message is inevitable
In this way, 139 recordings of soldiers of the British Air Forces reached the Internet, and more than 75,000 people have already looked at their "channel".
Britain's Ministry of Defence registered in the spring of 2007 as "defenceheadquarters" and publishes recordings of events in both Iraq and Afghanistan, while Britain's Commonwealth Office and international peacekeeping units in Iraq (MNFI) have their own channels on YouTube.
Deployment in Afghanistan
The Air Force has released videos according to their original intention for the purpose of recruitment and, according to the Ministry of Defense, are also good to familiarize the general public with the actual goal of the military's presence abroad.
"The promotion published in this form costs almost nothing compared to traditional television recruitment advertisements," Gordon Mackenzie, a defence expert, told the BBC, "but there is necessarily also a political message, because in a special way it confirms the legitimacy of the military actions decided by the government."
The Americans did the game
In addition to carefully edited, censored recordings, the army also receives amateur videos on the server, depicting, for example, the death of soldiers: in slightly hypocritical, "official" promotional films, the officers at most talk about losses, but there is no bloody form of combat.
After all, many videos from the battlefields in Iraq and Afghanistan, which were created during a sharp deployment, have already been released on the Internet, but the British army has only just discovered them.
The Americans recruit a little differently - using a shooting game called America's Army, which is free to download and in which the player can, through a more or less faithful simulation, get directly into the skin of the soldiers.
There'll be Christmas in ministries with raffloos and pampages
Labour Minister Mónika Lamperth is apparently not sentimental: instead of a Christmas party, she will hold a final working meeting for her collaborators, at which she will say goodbye to £2074.
In some ministries there will be a celebration with live music, in others it will conjure up the Christmas atmosphere with a raffle and toast.
The party, the Christmas atmosphere, the concerts and the Swedish table - that's how the mighty have fun.
There are also state agencies preparing for Christmas.
The Ministry of Defence will hold the most costlish ministerial Christmas celebration this year, but does not await his employees.
At Christmas, the children of the fallen soldiers will receive presents, during the Missionary Christmas they will treat the family members of the soldiers currently on foreign missions, but they will also organize a reception for journalists.
The resort devotes 25 million to the atmosphere of the end of the year.
The National Development Agency (NFÜ) and the Ministry of Economy and Transport (GKM) will throw themselves out of their hooves compared to other ministries.
The GKM party is held in the Jövő Háza centre, for a total of 4 320 000 forints, and out of the six hundred employees of the ministry, approximately three hundred and fifty guests are expected.
The resort will stick to the Christmas traditions at its celebration, but there will also be various performances and there will also be a raffle.
Christmas NFÜ cost 4 700 000 forints and took place on Wednesday in the building of the State Judicial Council (OIT) - concerts and dinner were on the agenda.
Renting the hall cost a million forints, the rest was spent on more than four hundred invited.
It seems that one NFÜ department is invited to another Christmas celebration: this will be organized by a company securing the course of tenders, the Hungarian Centre for Economic Development (MAG) from EU money.
For this purpose, they leased the Vígszínház Theatre building.
MAG a.s. did not confirm this information because we were unable to reach the relevant person.
There's no need a suit
The OIT building hosted not only NFÜ, but also the Ministry of Justice and Security, which held the St. Lucia Ball instead of Christmas on December 7.
OIT, however, didn't want money from it using its halls.
Five hundred people took part in the ball.
The party began with the minister's greetings, after dinner it was possible to dance accompanied by live music.
According to the answer to [origo]'s question at the ball, evening dresses or suits were not mandatory at all.
Puritan party is planned by health, labor and local government departments.
The Ministry of Health organizes a program and celebration for employees for 400 000 forints in its own building.
Minister of Labour Mónika Lamperthová does not even officially invite collaborators to the Christmas party, but at the final working meeting.
One hundred and eighty guests are counted, who, according to the press department, will only get coffee and pags, so only 350 000 forints will be enough for the party.
The Minister for Local Authorities Gordon Bajnai and his collaborators will simply toast in the auditorium of the Ministry of Local Government and Regional Development.
The Ministry of Finance (PM) will hold a Christmas reunion at which it expects 436 workers, in its own large hall.
At the Christmas party he devotes 2,500 forints per guest - the total amount includes a reception with Swedish tables and a toast.
Since he is Minister János Veres, PM workers celebrate Christmas in a similar way every year.
However, the atmosphere of the party will be absent, the officiality intensifies not only the ministerial speech, but also the afternoon time.
On January 20, the agriculture resort organizes a New Year's reception, ministerial Christmas does not take place.
The reception will take place in Vajdahunyadvár Castle in the Town Gardens and, according to the statement of the Ministry, 1 000 forints per person is intended for one hundred guests.
According to the press department, this will be a very simple reception, to which only relevant foreign diplomats accredited in Hungary are invited.
These annual receptions begin with a brief greeting of the minister and end with a minor treat.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs and the Ministry of Education and Culture do not hold Christmas parties at all, the relevant press departments told the [origo] portal.
They were unable to announce the price of Christmas celebrations of the environment department in the press department.
In any case, four hundred people were invited to a party held at the Museum of Decorative Arts.
There's a huge strike going on
On Friday, the Association of Trade Unions of the Liga, or the National Union of Workers’ Councils (MOSZ), will form its strike commission to coordinate Monday’s indefinite strike.
A protest public rally will be held on Saturday, on Monday - on the day of the final vote on the Health Insurance Act - work will be halted.
The action will certainly cause serious interruptions in transport, as rail traffic almost completely stops, and employees of the Budapest Transport Company (BKV) will also go on strike within 13 hours.
On Friday afternoon, the Liga association will introduce together the Nationwide Union of Workers' Boards with the details of a jointly organized Monday strike.
There will also be a strike committee LIGA - MOSZ, which will coordinate Monday's event.
There will be a protest public rally on Saturday, and from Monday the unions joining the strike will halt indefinitely if there is no agreement with the government.
This means there has not been a deadline to end the strike, which will begin on the day of the parliamentary vote on the health insurance law.
If Parliament does not accept the health insurance law in the end, the unions will withdraw the call for a strike immediately.
However, if the legislation is voted on, the strike committees will meet and decide on the next course of action.
According to Monday's agenda of Parliament's meeting, it is also possible that there will only be voting on the Health Insurance Act in the late evening or at night.
Due to Monday's strike, it is possible to expect serious interruptions of transport: on the railway, the work will be stopped for an indefinite period, in the morning, a part of Budapest's public transport and more than halve of Volán companies can stop.
At the same time, in many places of the country there will also be a closure of half of the lane.
Numerous unions and civic organisations, whose list is constantly updated on the Liga website, have joined the call for a strike.
Some organizations announced an indefinite strike, others declared a temporary halt to work.
By Thursday afternoon, the Hungarian state railways (MÁV) and the Independent Trade Union of Railway Employees (VDSZSZ) could not even agree on sufficient services, while at the beginning of the Christmas holidays, more and more people are traveling by rail.
According to the previous communication, from the beginning of operation until 13 o'clock there will be a strike at BKV, from 7 a.m
The Union of Hungarian Physicians will strike alternately across the country, the Democratic Trade Union of Workers in Health and Social (EDDSZ), belonging to the Co-Operation Forum of Trade Unions (SZEF), is holding a two-hour warning strike.
The most important of the four requirements formulated by the Liga association is for the government to withdraw the proposal for health insurance reform and submit a new concept for public discussion.
The second requirement is that the new method of counting pensions in force from next year should be introduced gradually.
In order to ensure the possibility of inclusion in the system before 2010 for early retirement for the relevant fields of employment.
The fourth reason for the event is that the Independent Trade Union of Railway Employees (VDSZSZ), belonging to the Liga trade union, does not accept the offer of the Ministry of Economy and Transport concerning railway branches, and expects a different offer.
Politial Capital: Hungarian Guard raises country's risk
According to the Risk Analysing Division of the Political Capital Institute, the activities of the Hungarian Guard, founded with the support of the Jobbik Magyarországért movement in August this year.
“In connection with the activities of the Hungarian Guard, it represents the greatest political risk that “the already existing ethnic tensions between the Roma and non-Romani peoples of Hungary can cause violent ethnic clashes within a short period of time.”
The main reason is that the political strategy of the Hungarian Guard is likely aimed at provoking such an event - the research institute adds.
As he writes, all this can be explained by one of the greatest negligences of Hungarian political representatives: "the public debate on the Roma issue was left by representatives of radical and extremist political groups, who were able to build a political strategy on the Romo-Hungarian contradiction."
This deepens the problem, prevents normal dialogue, strengthens extremists and increases the likelihood of creating violent conflicts - the institute judges.
In addition, according to the research institute, the activities of the Hungarian Guard have “to a large extent spoiled the image of Hungary abroad, especially in terms of faith in the stability of the country and its democratic maturity.”
The Institute recalls: part of the reorganization of Hungarian radical and far-right groups, which has received increasing publicity and vehemence since the autumn of 2006, is that several organizations have been formed in Hungary since the summer of 2007, which they call themselves "national militias" associations.
"The common feature of these groups is that they define themselves as civic associations, while they openly proclaim - even armed - the readiness of their members to perform defensive tasks (in the event of natural disasters, assault of the homeland, disruption of order), moreover, pointing to the incompetence of the police, the army, or the state," he adds.
He further notes: all of this happens in a way that seeks to wrap up all sorts of political efforts based on society's resistance to politics and parties into civilian robes.
Therefore, in the case of these groups, the emphasis of civility and the form of the association serves not only to avoid the legal pitfalls of judicial registration, but is also a political strategy.
According to the analysis, the activities of the Hungarian Guard are also a factor increasing the risk in terms of neighborly relations.
“In recent months they have referred to the Hungarian Guard in several neighbouring countries as a potential source of danger, ultra-nationalist political leaders of some neighboring countries want to excuse their own anti-Hungarian policy by appealing to the creation of the Hungarian Guard.
All of this can affect the political stability of the area," he adds.
According to the research institute, the formation of the Hungarian Guard in Central and Eastern Europe is not a unique phenomenon, so the political risks to the region as a whole have increased.
As he writes, the activities of the Hungarian Guard are similar, for example, to the activities of the Bulgarian National Guard, the creation of which was announced in August 2007 by the ultra-right Bulgarian National Union (BNU).
Against the background of the establishment of the Bulgarian Guard stands the mass Romani riots that broke out in one of the Sofia neighborhoods (Krasna Poljana).
The BNU used it to establish a guard, saying the organization's goal was a "national militia."
For the past 17 years there has been "Gypsy terror" in Bulgaria, but the state has remained idle and indifferent, recalls the Political Capital Institute.
He adds that the initiative has sparked tumultuous discussion from the Bulgarian public.
As he goes on, the increase in the risk of ethnic clashes signals the announcement by some Bulgarian Romani organizations that they will create their own guard in a sign of resistance.
Since the activities of the Hungarian Guard show similarities to the activities of the Bulgarian National Guard, it is not excluded that even Hungarian Roma will respond to the threat in a similar way, the establishment of their own militia, we can read in the risk warning prepared by the Political Capital Institute.
When asked by the press office, Attila Juhász, the research director of the Political Capital Institute: risk analysis is one of the activities of the research institute.
As he said, they issue risk analysis every year, and if they find out something that could have an effect on the risk change, they inform public opinion, as in this case, by issuing risk warnings.
Holidays aren't free everywhere
In Hungary, the days off are precisely stipulated by law and it has already become a general practice that weekdays are moving around the holidays, which awaits us fewer times in the next year.
Holidays are not uniform in the European Union or in America.
The Hungarian legal system distinguishes between national and public holidays and days of rest.
Days of working rest are mostly related to church holidays or important historical events.
We also celebrate the New Year: the last day of the old and the first day of the new year is time off - on the New Year so that people can relax after New Year's Eve.
During the year, we have a total of eleven free holidays.
The national holiday is March 15 celebrating the Revolution of 1848 and October 23, the commemorative day of the 1956 Revolution.
On August 20 we celebrate the founding of the state, on May 1 work, on November 1 and 2 we remember deceased relatives and friends.
In addition, there are two-day holidays of Easter, Pentecost and Christmas.
Since our national, national or religious holidays rarely fall on the weekend according to the calendar, it has become a practice to move the working day in cases where we would work only one day before or after the holiday.
However, the price for long weekends is that previous Saturdays we remain in the workplace and at school.
The Minister of Labour has been adjustinging for many years in a special decree when the day immediately precedes or is followed.
The legislation applies equally to all employers and workers employed by them, to public employees as well as to persons in public service.
However, it does not apply to companies and institutions that are in operation without interruption or, with regard to their determination, operate even on days of rest.
This year there will be a change in the working order just around the holidays at the end of the year: from December 24, life in the country will almost stop, because according to previous experience, most of the days between Christmas and New Year's Eve will "work" in advance or thus take advantage of the spared holiday.
Next year, several of our holidays fall in the middle, respectively, at the end of the week, so there will be less movement of the working day than this year.
For the first time, the calendar will be transformed around Labor Day, so that workers can then stay at home four days tastefully.
Saturday, April 26, will be a working day, so that Friday, May 2, can be a day of rest.
The next move is related to a holiday on October 23: on Saturday, October 18, there will be work instead of Friday 24.
However, the end of the year will be able to take place completely in the sign of the holidays, because from December 24 they will have almost everywhere nonstop holidays, even though New Year's Eve falls on Wednesday.
Six deterrent little things to upset your colleagues with
The e-mails we forget to send, the voice messages we never receive, innocent lies that we almost don't even notice anymore: all this "diversifies" weekdays in the workplace.
The question is whether you will get used to these minor inconveniences, or if you put on your gloves and unclear situations, which others can become angry, you will deliberately avoid.
Tips.
"I'm sorry, I forgot to tell you they were looking for you from the marketing department," a colleague tells you.
That you missed the meeting because of it?
In the best case, he regrets it and tries to fix it, or calls the marketing department and says he forgot to pass the message (this is a less common solution).
Most often, the matter is resolved with one apology.
It is good to think about how you can prepare unpleasant moments for your colleagues and consciously try to prevent it.
Do you borrow something that isn’t yours?
There's nothing more uncomfortable than when you reach out for a piece of writing, an office need or for your phone, and they're not there.
Do you remember you put a stapler on the table yesterday, but it's gone by morning?
Or did you discover on the desk of one of your colleagues your pen, which you considered lost?
Don't take anything from your colleagues without permission, otherwise they'll be rightly angry about you that you're digging into things.
Do not remove envelopes or sticky papers from the office, because it can be unpleasant if you run out prematurely, and you will need them immediately.
Gossip about the private life of colleagues?
There's not a good solution to stopping spreading gossips.
Who, with whom, what was he doing?
Where and why?
Don't hang everyone's messages (presumptions) about the private lives of colleagues!
If one of them shares with you their innermost secrets, do not abuse his trust!
Lock in yourself what you have heard, and do not discuss it with your colleagues in his absence, even when others also know about his things.
For no reason, do you report your absence?
If you call the office and, citing a severe headache, report your absence because you are not feeling well, or if you think that today you would rather rest, remember that your work, urgent tasks and work that have a certain deadline will have to be done for you by someone else.
Consider your colleagues!
If you know in advance that you won’t go to work the next day, or that something can happen that you’ll have to stay at home, please report that your colleagues and leaders won’t be confronted with your absence until the next day.
Hunching, giggling, making a phone call
Have you immersed yourself in the work from which the giggling of your colleagues will tear you out?
Have you accidentally witnessed a telephone conversation that would be better not heard at all?
Do not disturb your co-workers by humming, singing, making a loud call (over their heads), or stomping, a constant curse, loud dissatisfaction.
All of this can be disruptive even when they are not involved in a project (writing, planning, organization).
Are you blaming others for your mistakes?
If something is not in time or you have forgotten to send an important letter or message, do not accuse others for your forgetfulness!
Don't refer to any chaos reigninginging in the office and noisy colleagues!
Acknowledge if you've made a mistake and someone's paid for it, and correct the harm!
Ask how you could put the matter in order, correct the negligence!
Do you feel too at home in your office?
Sweatpants and slippers: comfortable clothes at home, but they are not suitable for the office!
Don’t wear your clothes, even if your profession allows it!
Don't be negligent, honor your colleagues and yourself by coming to the office in a regular dress - and if your profession requires it, nicely and elegantly!
If you want to suit, you don't get interesting
Driving in the usual morning constipation.
There is a special melody from the radio: the sound of the violin, to which the electronic effects connect.
I'm adding volume.
The music changes the pace, and to the previous tonal tinting is added bass, then as a real jewel joins the women's choir.
At mr2 station, Zagar's song called Wings of Love.
For a long time, domestic musical life hasn’t spoiled us with high-quality electronic pop.
All of a sudden, however, constipation looks quite different, because you're just listening to Zagaru's new album.
Yonderboia's revelation, a pair of new and interesting productions coming from the home's digital underground are slowly changing the overall picture, and although it's fashionable garage rock with its beating into the guitar again, even domestic electropop proves there's something new under the sun.
Balázs Zságer started in the Fifth Church and he also played in Yonderboi's band, until finally - perhaps because two bagpipes can't fit into one charda - he went his own way.
However, he didn ́t break through with his project (Zagar) and music, which combines elements of jazz, electronic dance styles and pop (e.g.
film by Ference Török "Szezon").
His formations, starring in smaller clubs, got to know a wider audience after wan2 magazine ranked their first album from 2002 among the 50 most important domestic albums and declared Zagar at Yonderboie as almost the only domestic entities determining developments that could be marketable even in the West.
Zagar and his band are without a doubt "on course", although, of course, no one asked them if they were satisfied with this label.
The band reacted how they belonged: they made a new, better album.
The now-released album Cannot Walk Fly Rather may open new paths for them, although the title itself is trying to create a follow-up with the initial flights in the Five-Church.
It is one verse from the composition of the old band Balázs Zságer.
A reference to etheric, flying would be in place, but in addition to the etherical touch of this music, there has now appeared - in accordance with the spirit, the trend of the times - a louder guitar, the influence of rock and roll.
In these songs, singer György Ligeti, currently working in England with the band The Puzzle, presented himself at the premiere of the record in Millenáris Park.
The album also produced one real radio hit, thanks to Mr2 Petőfi, which has been playing popular music from morning to evening, we have started to experience the sweeping melodies of Wings of Love, recorded with six domestic singers ("Underground Divas").
So everything's fine (?).
Hvg.hu interviewed Balázs Zságer.
hvg.hu: In connection with this new hit, the question arises as to how muculous you play remains?
Balázs Zságer: The definition of marginal music doesn't mean much to me.
In my opinion, there are two different castes of light music; not mainstream and underground, not an alternative and pop, but commercial and honest.
Even the so-called alternative, underground music can be flat, they don't say anything, and just like that, it can arise just for the sake of sales, which makes it commercial.
However, even a good pop composition can stem from sincere internal needs.
In this spirit, the attitude as it is adopted works, regardless of language and national borders.
hvg.hu: Do you see any difference in the openness of the Hungarian and foreign audience to your music?
B. Zs.: In foreign, especially from us to the west, light music has greater traditions.
Not only teenagers and people in their twenties, but also older ones don't go to concerts there.
They are much more watched by the transformations of the light music around them, because they also regard it as part of contemporary culture.
hvg.hu: What is the role of the audience in your work?
Do you care who, where and how much he listens to your music?
B. Zs.: Of course, it feels good that more and more people are coming to our concerts.
But we've never made music for the market.
In our country, music is not born with a goal, but for some reason.
The role of the audience can be perceived rather at concerts, where mutual influence already works, but it hides only in the intangible changes of the moment.
Nevertheless, I noticed that if a person wants to suit the audience, from that moment on, it ceases to be interesting.
hvg.hu: To what extent are you going to follow the example of Yonderboi?
He works solo and has built up his career rather from us to the west.
B. ZS.: We do not actually go his way, regardless of the fact that they sometimes try to explain our music.
We're more like team-mades relying on each other's musical presence and letting it develop.
At first, of course, it was impossible not to compare our works to the songs of Yonderboie, because several of us separated from his band and took a different path.
hvg.hu: In the toplist station mr2 Petőfi, the song Wings of Love came first.
Many have heard the name Zagar for the first time in connection with this song.
In a more popular way, did you consciously open yourself up to wider layers?
B.Zs.: We didn't plan to write a hit by which we would sell the album.
There's more going on in that song.
We intended it as the finale of the album, by which we tried to unleash the previous sound orgy and more complex themes.
hvg.hu: How did you choose the six singers?
Why is it - what was the guiding principle?
B.Zs.: The idea was to create such a gospel choir.
Then came the idea to assemble this choir from the soloists of the domestic music scene with a good voice.
That's how this six-member choir came together - Sena, Edin Kutzor, Judie Jay, Bori Péterfy, Enikő Hodosi [Neo], Juci Németh -, which we then christened on Underground Divas.
hvg.hu: This is exactly how you intended the song, or did the girls reshape something about it?
B.Zs.: The song has long existed in the instrumental version, when the material on the board was met, we thought that there would still be something needed for this song.
A sequence of chords tempted him to be joined by a singing part.
Even that night I sang a tune on him, which the girls later learned.
It all came out almost the first time and terribly spontaneous.
I remember when it came back to life, but of course it took Wonders.
It wouldn't have worked that vigorously with my voice.
hvg.hu: Both on the album and at the concert seemed to be girls in the background.
They don't have a solo (except Judy Jay), they don't have their own role, they sing completely together and they're not louder than music, so the music doesn't just accompany their singing.
They also stood in the back on stage.
That intentionally?
B.Zs.: That they stood in the back was only because of technical problems.
That we didn't want to divide solo roles, on the other hand, is intentional.
Even the song is about forgetting a little about our ego, from where, where they belong, and just letting ourselves be lifted by the wings of love.
I wished that this euphoric state, which we felt when the song was born, would be felt by others.
hvg.hu: With which was the most work?
B.Zs.: During composing the music actually with not one, heavier pieces occurred more when mixing.
For example, Dorian Gray's Dilemma, in which I play on a concert wing, or Rock'n'Roll Goes To The Girls, where he didn't want to work groove for a long time, then everything slowly found its place like in a jigsaw puzzle.
hvg.hu: Which song from the new album is your favorite?
B.Zs.: That's always changing with touring and because I've already released the album.
Now it's Prophet Is A Fool, Escape From The Earth and Never The Same.
Treacherous broker risks billions of inferiority
Treacherous defraudant.
That's how French banking giant Société Générale, the owner of a domestic Komerční banka, refers to its fabled employee Jerome Kerviel.
She lost almost five billion euros because of it.
Rather, however, Kerviel appears to have been just a big player who wanted to cope with colleagues and, moreover, certainly not incompetent.
In contrast to all the big banking houses, he recognized in time a crisis in the U.S. mortgage market.
"He's mentally weaker," said his superiors, who had sent police to him.
But according to Kerviel’s testimony brought by the French media, such as the daily Le Monde, it was a man who made 1.6 billion euros of profits from his trades at the end of last year.
Crises not only in America
"All I wanted was to make money for my bank," he argues in testimony.
In the summer, he managed to make half a billion euros from the U.S. crisis.
He sensed the opportunity at the beginning of the year.
In February, reports began that so-called sub-prime mortgages, provided to low-income clients or those who have had repayment problems in the past, would have problems.
However, this crisis should not have had an impact on the whole economy.
Kerviel didn't believe it and bet correctly.
At the end of the year, its profit climbed to 1.6 billion euros.
"Even when I made the first half-billion, I didn't know how to announce it," Kerviel admits, adding that he certainly didn't keep anything for himself.
Throughout the interrogation, he admits that he falsified records in such a way that it doesn't come.
He even managed to deceive the bosses, even though Eurex became interested in his trades.
The risk was a gain
Was he afraid he'd come?
Rather, it's not.
"I thought only the simple fact that I've made $1.6 billion for a bank will satisfy them."
Where did he get the will to take so much risks?
He had to gradually develop into his position.
He joined Société Générale in 2000 just after he graduated from the University of Lyon.
His annual salary was about 51 thousand dollars.
Two years later, he was already an assistant to the broker, and in 2005 he became a stand-alone broker with an annual salary of 90 thousand dollars.
Still, he knew he'd never earn as much as others.
"Even at my first interview in 2005, I knew they were looking at me.
Because of my education and also because of my professional and personal development," Kerviel described to investigators.
But shortly after boarding came the first great achievement.
Thanks to Allianz's shares, he earned half a billion euros in bombings on London.
And that was the triggering mechanism - he wanted more.
As long as you're earning, we'll leave you
"It's like a snower ball," he told police.
But the bosses knew about everything, or rather suspected it.
"As long as I was earning money, they turned a blind eye."
But then came the blow at the beginning of this year.
He lost a billion euros in a single day.
After the weekend in mid-January, he wanted to cover it up.
When he came to work on Monday, he was no longer working at the bank.
And the bank reported a few days later that it lost five billion euros because of it.
And how did they find him?
He became a suspect because of his vacation.
"I've only chosen four days for the past year.
A broker who doesn't have a holiday is a broker who doesn't want anyone looking into his records," Kerviel concluded.
The Czech Republic has a chance of winning 365 billion
Ministers of the Czech government signed a total of nine operational programmes with representatives of the European Commission yesterday, of which the Czech Republic can receive 14 billion euros (approximately 365 billion crowns) in the coming years.
It is about half of the total amount that the Czech Republic can receive from Brussels between 2007 and 2013.
Approximately 125 billion crowns will go from seven operational programmes to the regions.
Czech companies will be able to receive around 100 billion crowns from the Entrepreneurship and Innovation programme.
Most money, up to £150bn, will go into transportation.
The Transport programme is the largest operational programme and represents up to 22 percent of the funds that the Czech Republic can receive from the funds.
This money will increase the budget of the State Transport Infrastructure Fund, which will manage the amount of 90.8 billion crowns next year.
Entrepreneurship and Innovation programme is intended to serve entrepreneurs and companies to raise funds for innovative entrepreneurship.
Firms should raise funds for the introduction of new technologies, for industrial research, but also for the introduction of more effective organizational methods.
So far, the Czech Republic is among the least successful in drawing money from EU funds within the newly adopted countries.
Twenty-year sentence for ex-president Nicaragua was upheld by the court
An appeals court in Nicaragua on Thursday confirmed a 20-year sentence for the country's former president, Arnold Aleman, to whom he was sentenced in 2003 for financial fraud amounting to $100 million (nearly 1.8 billion crowns).
However, he was later released from prison and lived under house arrest on his hacienda near the Nicaraguan metropolis.
It is not clear whether the former head of this Central American state from 1997 to 2020 will go back to prison, according to the Associated Press.
According to Ileana Pérezová, the president of the Court of Appeal, it will now depend on prison officials whether to allow Aleman to serve his sentence at home or go to prison.
Four years ago, the court proved to Aleman that he regularly shifted government money to the accounts of foreign businesses in Panama.
These companies were fictitious, and the account actually belonged to his relatives and closest followers.
Some of this money then ended up in a fund that Aleman founded to finance his election campaign in 2001.
That's how they approved the budget
That's the way it is.
The state budget has been adopted.
What makes this budget special?
It is the first budget drawn up by this government and adopted by a coalition majority without the support of the opposition.
He went through last year with the support of the ČSSD, because he was prepared by Minister Sobotka and the ČSSD could not afford his non-acceptance.
Unlike the bustling debate ahead of a no-confidence vote for the government, nothing dramatic happened this time.
The Chamber of Deputies passed the bill calmly.
It's a paradox.
Every government begins and ends with individual chapters and budget items, whether someone likes it or doesn’t like it.
The budget is an essential part of politics, but it is hidden behind the government's power rivalry with the opposition.
This is more grateful in the media, which is which to pay much more attention to it in the media.
Fighting by politicians is far more interesting to the public than austere numbers.
State budgets in past social democratic governments have suffered from three ailments, which it is not able to suddenly solve for 2008:
The first of them is a high proportion of the law of compulsory, so-called mandatory expenditures, which are not only pensions, but also social benefits, which were approved last year before the elections by a quiet "coalition" of the ČSSD and the KSČM.
The second is deficit management as a result of the first ailment.
The third limit is public deficits, i.e. the deficit of budgets of cities and municipalities.
However, the biggest risk is not the state budget deficit.
It is public and relatively under control.
However, a time bomb is a deficit in the budgets of cities and municipalities.
Many mayors, in addition to money from taxes, fees and loans from banks, get additional resources for investments, often recklessly issuing bonds by which they disproportionately indebted their town halls.
In recent years, they have managed to repay these debts through the mass sale of shares of energy and other power grids they have received from the state in privatisation.
They no longer have this resource.
If we stick to only official and verifiable facts, we will find that:
Our economy has been going through a growth phase for several years, like the rest of the world, thus constantly bragging about the ČSSD.
This was a unique opportunity for its governments to put public finances in order.
Instead, however, the state became increasingly indebted.
Other countries, with few exceptions such as Hungary, Albania, Pakistan or Egypt, have farmed much more sensibly than our republic.
It's a huge embarrassment that's falling primarily on past governments.
We are among the countries that, on a global scale, are the least threatened by poverty.
Therefore, there is no need for massive social programs, which, unfortunately, in recent years quite unnecessarily swelled to gigantic proportions.
Even if it managed to abolish some of the senseless populist expenditures approved this year before the elections, if in the near future there is no way to shift the limit of retirement and pension reform, then effectively any policy in this country will end, and no matter who governs, it will be mere office.
With little exaggeration, it can be said that the ruling politicians will become officials, mechanically dividing the majority of taxed money into pensions and social benefits.
That's why this year's budget can't be revolutionary, but it can only suspend the displacement of normal spending by mandators.
A planned deficit of ££70bn for 2008 would have been completely unnecessary if past governments hadn't managed what they could in the last ten years.
If we do not really want the ruling politicians to become only officials, then we have to be patient and indulge in this not very strong Coalition government, which realizes that the state can issue only as much as it can get in taxes and fees, peace to prepare further reform laws.
Only these will allow for the gradual achievement of a balanced state budget.
Jiri Krejci
Banks want to solve the crisis
The U.S. Federal Reserve Bank, the European Central Bank and the central banks of Britain, Switzerland and Canada have decided to jointly combat the crisis in the financial markets.
More than a hundred billion dollars will reach the currency markets through auctions.
In these auctions, the bank auctions an interest rate for which they are willing to borrow money.
For example, the U.S. Fed sends forty billion dollars to auction, another twenty-four to be made available to European and Swiss central banks.
It is the first time after the attacks of the eleventh of September that national central banks have agreed on such a coordinated procedure to prevent or reduce the consequences of the financial crisis.
"This move is intended to address the economy's greatest difficulties, i.e. the growing lack of free capital that banks could lend to," Bernard Baumol of the consulting firm Economic Outlook Group wrote in his report.
"Coordinated action by central banks reduced the likelihood of a recession in the United States from 65 percent to about fifty percent," Baumol added in his report.
Banks have already lost sixty billion dollars due to non-performing loans, primarily in the United States.
Analysts expect even more billion-dollar losses.
"Central banks must be really afraid of what else can happen when they've decided on this procedure," ABN chief economist Amro Kieran Davies said.
"It looks like the situation is really bad," he added.
Central banks are concerned that if banks themselves have difficult access to loans, they willy lend heavily to their customers.
This would reduce consumption and send the U.S. economy into a recession with possible impacts on the world's economic growth.
Central banks thus decided, after failing to lower the so-called LIBOR rate, the London interbank rate used for borrowing money in the interbank market.
This meant that banks were still worried about borrowing money from each other, even though the U.S., Canadian and British central banks cut their interest rates several times during the fall.
"It will have the same effect as a further fall in interest rates," Greg Salvaggio of Tempus Consulting told Reuters.
Some analysts don't think the central bank's move will contribute to igniting the crisis because it doesn't address the underlying problem, and that's the downfall of the U.S. property market.
Klaus: Ecologists' debates are unbelievable
Global climate change is supposed to hurt developing countries the most.
President Vaclav Klaus once again commented on the issue of global warming.
Criticism has now descended on the end-of-the-clock international U.N
In Vienna, where he presented his book The Blue, Not Green Planet, he stated that the debates of environmentalists are incredible.
According to Klaus, not only the structure of the conference, which consists of a strange mixture of representatives of governments and NGOs, but also the fundamental difference between the rhetoric that accompanies it and the reality is unacceptable.
"The debate there is completely unstructured, it's just fireworks of words that are a substitute for solving current problems," Klaus said.
When presenting his book, the Czech president emphasized that its theme is very important to him, and that is why he is so involved for them.
He also expressed satisfaction that the book also appears in German translation, because, as he said, "I often only encounter caricatures of my views."
Now those interested can read his undistorted attitudes.
Klaus reminded that although he is not a climatologist, in his opinion he has enough prerequisites to write such a book.
He is an academic, able to monitor and assess professional treatises, has experience with statistics, and is also an economist.
As the main conclusions of the book, he called the thesis that the climate is constantly changing, and its changes are therefore normal, that there is no consensus on the warming of the planet of all scientists, that the consequences of current changes are not very large and serious and therefore do not pose a threat, and that efforts to influence the development of the climate are unnecessary, ineffective and unacceptably costly.
Moreover, as a person with experience with totalitarianism, he considers it unacceptable to impose unproven opinions on others, which he feels as a dangerous restriction of freedom.
When asked by those present, he repeatedly explained that it was not against "reasonable protection of the environment, but against militant environmentalism, which does not take into account the relationship between costs and their benefits."
On the same topic, Klaus spoke in Vienna two months ago at the Hayek Institute.
The driver's license was not exchanged by 600 thousand people
Six hundred thousand drivers missed the deadline and did not manage to exchange an old driver's license, which expires at the end of the year.
Today is the last day when people can apply for the exchange, so that the authorities can still issue them a new card by the end of the year.
However, the Ministry is not afraid that hundreds of thousands of drivers would go on the road after the New Year without a valid license.
According to the authorities, they did not exchange the card mainly those who do not even want to drive anymore.
While municipalities are reporting a sharp drop in interest in the exchange of the driver's license, authorities in the metropolis are overwhelmed by applications, and interest in exchanging a card this week has risen.
The magistrate handles about 2000 clients a day.
There's about half an hour in the queues.
Approximately 140,000 drivers have not exchanged their driver's licenses in Prague.
It takes twenty days to issue a new card.
However, it is possible to apply for extradition within a reduced period of five days.
In this case, however, the driver pays five hundred crowns.
Motorists who do not have a mandatory exchange by the end of the year, and drive out on the road without a valid driver's license, will face a fine from January.
The offender will pay a maximum of two thousand crowns.
However, a police officer can also deal with an agreement.
In the case of an administrative offense, the fine will climb to 2500 crowns.
According to previous information, even a person who is not going anywhere could receive a fine from the town hall.
He was supposedly able to pay 30,000 crowns.
The ministry has previously urged the authorities to be moderate and impose a maximum of a thousand-crown fine.
According to the spokesperson of the Ministry of Transport Karel Hanzelka, however, tens of thousands of fines do not threaten anyone.
According to him, the driver's license is a document for which people can decide whether they want it or not.
It's not an identity card that every person must have.
People who don't want to drive anymore should give up their driver's license.
All you have to do is take it or send it by registered mail to the competent municipal office.
With the end of the year, cards issued between 1964 and 1993 will cease to apply.
However, motorists have to gradually exchange other types of driver's licenses.
People with documents from 1994 to 2000 still have three years to exchange.
Cards issued between 2001 and 2004 will expire in December 2013.
According to Hanzelka, there are about 2.6 million other documents.
Court reduced sentences for detectives linked to Berdych
Former police officers Josef Opava and Petr Koňařík, who are accused of a criminal conspiracy with the David Berdych gang, will go to prison for thirteen and ten years.
This was decided definitively by the Prague High Court, which reduced their sentences by one by a year and the other by two years.
Initially, detectives of the Prague Department for Organized Crime were 14 and 12 years old.
Today's decision can no longer be appealed, it is only possible to appeal to the Supreme Court.
Berdych is also indicted in this case, but he is only waiting for a first-instance verdict.
In other trials, however, he has already been given sentences in sums exceeding 16 years.
He can't get any more.
In January, the regional court acknowledged, in particular, of involvement in robberies involving 19 people, the High Court today dealt with an appeal of 16 of them.
Most of the defendants have denied the indictment since the beginning of the investigation, saying it was a fabricated case.
According to the Central Bohemian Senate, members of the police, in agreement with Berdych, handed over information from police sources to the criminals, covered up individual robberies and helped members of the gang to avoid criminal liability.
For this, the gang handed over 30 percent of the magnifying glass for Opava and Koňařík.
The indictment involved the criminal activity of the gang from 1999 to 2001.
Particularly the robbery, whose victims often suffered serious bodily harm, but also about the abuse of the authority of a public official and illicit armament.
During house searches, the police discovered illegally held pistols, bullets with a steel core, a laser sight or a submachine gun.
According to the charges, the gang came by robbery for tens of millions of crowns.
In addition to nearly twenty men in this trial, the courts have already pleaded guilty to other Berdych robberies and a number of other people.
Other men around Berdych are still pleading before the trial in another trial, some cases have not yet been brought before the court.
So far, Tomáš Půta and Maroš Šulej, who are residing in Irish custody, are waiting for a final decision on their extradition for prosecution in the Czech Republic.
Children's subscrution account: 12 million
Igor Veleba, a lawyer for damaged families, wants compensation of 12 million crowns, plus court costs, after the Třebíč hospital.
For each of the mothers after three million, for a father of two million and for children by a million.
The hospital offers a maximum of 200,000 for each parent.
Ondøøej Dostál, the hospital’s legal representative, therefore fundamentally disagrees with Veleby’s proposal.
"We reject such a request, it is very high," Dostál said after today's meeting.
"But in order to have an opinion about the counter-proposal, we need an expert opinion, which the opposing party has commissioned.
Let's see if it's worthwhile at all.
However, we continue to believe that it will be in hundreds of thousands for everyone," Dostál emphasized.
According to him, the amount should range between 100,000 to 200,000 crowns for each of the four parents.
At the beginning of the case, a family from Jabloňov wanted a million crowns for every month, when she did not raise her biological daughter.
Veleba served as the basis for the negotiation of compensation an expert opinion prepared for him by the University Hospital in Prague-Motola.
According to experts in psychology and psychiatry, parents of children have suffered psychological harm and stress in various degrees and depths.
The stressful situation was better tolerated by men, although they were struggling with regrets that they did not prevent the confusion of newborns.
In turn, women find it harder to cope with the actuality of the trauma experienced, and with what's happening right now, he's summed up the content of Veleba.
Veronika and Nikola were replaced by nurses in the Třebíč hospital on 9 December last year.
Last week the children returned to their biological parents - Nikola to Čermáková in Přibyslavice and Veronika to Libor Brož and Jaroslava Trojanova to Jabloňov.
At the end of October, Miloš Vystrčil handed over 100,000 crowns to each of the families to compensate for the expenses incurred by the mistake of the Třebíč hospital established by the region.
All hospitals in the Czech Republic are legally insured under liability for damage.
In the event of misconduct, they must reach into the reserve fund, exceptionally they can apply for the help of the founder.
So far, the courts have awarded relatively low compensation to people, the sums have been counted according to tables in the order of hundreds of thousands, exceptionally millions of crowns.
Polívka lost the Wallachian Kingdom
Bolek Polívka lost a dispute over the Wallachian Kingdom brand.
According to the judge of the Ostrava Regional Court for the first time, he used the designation Wallachian king for the first time, but the project was built by a team around Tomáš Harabiš, who cooperated with the actor.
Polívka did not participate in negotiations today.
According to his lawyer, however, the case is probably not over and the client will appeal.
"The dispute is that Mr Harabiš and his team parasitize the popularity of Bolek Polívka," Polívka's lawyer said after the trial.
According to him, at the words of the Wallachian Kingdom, everyone remembers actors and comedians.
Harabiš has the same opinion.
"There's almost a ten-year job behind us.
Looking for money and inventing actions," he said.
According to him, the cause of the dispute is money.
"After years, they've realised that the project is successful and he could get something out of it.
They want to parasitize on our work," he added.
Lucrative business
According to the court, for the first time, the Wallachian king used Polívka on his TV show.
It was crowned in 1993 by actor Radoslav Brzobohatý.
At the time, however, it had nothing to do with the intention of building a fictional kingdom.
A few years later, Harabiš came up with the idea of issuing Wallachian passports.
The original recessionist action turned out to be a business and a lucrative business activity, to which Harabiš continued the establishment of the Wallachian Kingdom company.
In 1997, Polívka agreed to put its name and signature on passports and even offered their holders discounts when visiting their farm and theatrical performances.
Who's going to take the throne?
The judge also said today that Harabiš only took advantage of the bare idea that Polívka published in management for his business plan.
"The popularity of Boleslav Polívka has certainly helped the Wallachian Kingdom project," the judge said.
She added, however, that Harabiš and his team, which devoted themselves intensively to him, were the soul of the project.
The cooperation of today's disputed parties continued until 2000, when Harabiš organized an official coronation for Polívka in Vsetín.
Since then, however, disputes began to accumulate between Polívka and Harabiš, culminating in the deposal of "King of Boleslav I., chosen at all times".
The leading role in the kingdom was then taken by singer Jarmila Šuláková as a queen mother at the instigation of Harabiš.
In 2002, Polívka filed an action against Harabiš and his company, which was dismissed by the court today.
Clear could bring Harabiš's forthcoming contest for the throne and half of the princess.
A new king should come out of it.
He didn't want to divulge the details and deadline.
“We don’t want to rush anything.
It should be kind of like the ride of kings in Slovakia.
The king should be elected for a period of time.
Even Mr. Polívka can easily apply," Harabiš added.
Test Peugeot 207 SW 1.6 VTi: disappearinging prejudices
In fact, I've never cheered too much for the Peugeot 206.
Its driving characteristics were not much, the quality of shifting, steering and also production could sometimes be described as below average.
But there's now a successor to the Peugeot 207, and it's much better.
And the station wagon called SW goes a little further and I have no choice but to think better.
When I tested the Peugeot 207's classic hatchback, I complained, for example, about the space on my head in the rear seats.
That's the end of these complaints here, because the station wagon's body means that even two-metre-longs won't suffer in this direction.
The Flight of the World
I'll go over the interior very quickly so I don't repeat too much.
This is a pretty good design, where I have the only reproach to the poor center tunnel.
Here I would appreciate a little more design invention, when the car already looks so elegant.
The seats are comfortable and there are times when we were able to complain about short seats or backrests.
Also, the lateral guidance improved significantly against the 206 model.
In terms of space, there is an increase in the dimensions of the body.
In the stumble, the aforementioned lack of space overhead disappears.
Suitcases in the suitcase
The main thing about this car is its luggage.
I have to evaluate it as very easily accessible (also thanks to tilting the rear window) and as decently stored.
Deaf spots are equipped with restraining rubbers, which are an ideal compromise.
It doesn't stop, it's easy to stretch while keeping even heavier objects.
But let's think about the volume together.
According to the tables in the basic arrangement 428 liters, which is more of an average value against the competition.
However, not against the best-selling car of this class in the Czech Republic - the Škoda Fabia Combi.
If we compare 207 SW with the new generation of the Mladá Boleslav product, then we will find that Peugeot lags by a very noticeable 52 liters.
For holidays abroad or when loading a stroller, this value is critical.
But if I had to highlight the positives, I still have to mention a completely flat floor after folding the backrests of the second row or a very low threshold.
120 horses in four rows
Our tested car was equipped with a 1.6-litre petrol four-cylinder engine with an output of 88 kW.
And to tell you the truth, that's what a sixteen-hundred-year-old should look like.
It's quiet, it has little consumption and, above all, it pulls.
If I compare it to the Škoda equivalent, which sometimes acts as if it has a stuffed rasp into the exhaust, I can only be thrilled about it.
The price list suggests
The price of the car is not popular.
However, we have to keep in mind that we tested a well-equipped car with a spacious interior and a very good engine.
The basic price of a car with a petrol fourteen hundred for 329 900 Kč will still pass.
Our aggregate costs from 410 900 Kč, which is already quite enough.
Tested equipment Premium will then throw in equipment for an additional 80 000 Kč on top.
But we still had head airbags, automatic air conditioning, automatic wiper start, electric control of the rear windows...
The car costs everything 531 900 Kč.
It's a lot, but count the additional equipment at Fabia and you'll come to a very similar number.
All we have to do is decide on the brand.
The integration of Slovak pupils at British schools is not going well
There is an increase in the number of children from the countries of Eastern Europeans in British schools, which raises concerns about the further development of local education.
So far, the integration of Eastern Europeans has encountered a number of obstacles.
British schools are even registering instances where controversies between immigrants and the children of indigenous people are turning violent.
The problem of integrating children from Eastern Europe at British schools is well illustrated by the case in northern Kent, where disputes between local settlers and Slovak immigrants are beginning to escalate.
Arguments, bullyings and brawls between students were also transferred into relationships between parents.
The situation went so far that in Chatham, police detained a Slovak woman who was beating a ten-year-old pupil with an iron rod.
The guilty defends that the boy insulted her with racist abuse.
Police had to intervene even after the attack of two teen-year-old girls on a Slovak immigrant.
The girls allegedly framed the man with racist remarks and threw rocks at him.
"When we went home, a boy climbed to a bus stop and started spitting at us.
When I told him to stop it, he swung apples and pears at us, and then exposed his butt," the mother of Slovak pupils told The Times.
Complaints are from both sides.
One of the local residents even called disputes with immigrants from Eastern Europe as a struggle for survival.
The above-mentioned cases indicate that the atmosphere in British schools between locals and immigrants is beginning to become tense.
In Britain, 70 thousand Slovaks are registered, who headed here for work.
Roughly 30,000 Slovak pupils attend British schools.
Cuba the prettiest island in the Caribbean
For visitors, it's really an island of freedom.
Whatever you expect from your vacation, it can come true if you make the right choice.
Golden and whitish beaches, high waves, or the sea, calm as a pond.
Palm trees and all-inclusive with waiters offering pizza, ice cream and intoxicating cocktails on the beach.
Kitesurfing, golf and roulette.
On islands in the Caribbean Sea you will find it in a complete and even richer list.
But only on one island you will find much more and the island is Cuba, the largest and prettiest of the Great Antilles.
Under the Spaniards, Cuba used to be the bastion of their conquestion of America.
Bolumus described it as the most beautiful place the human eye has ever seen.
Havana became a storehouse and transshipmentable of the treasures the Spaniards robbed and transported to Europe.
And then came the American era.
Hotel Nacional grew up at the very end of the 1920s.
It was built by one of U.S. mafia bosses Lucky Luciano, because everything that was a crime in the U.S. (especially during Prohibition) was greeted in Cuba and supported by drugs, gambling, prostitution.
Among his guests were, among others.
Winston Churchill, Ava Gardner, Frank Sinatra.
High-rise houses were built in Havana, there were cars on wide boulevards, which belonged to the iconic symbols of the 50's of the last century.
Ernest Hemingway boughted a homestead in San Francisco de Paula, near Havana, and had his yacht in Cojimar.
On the walls of Bodeguity del Medio, even politicians from all over the Americas left their signatures.
What about today?
You can leave your autograph on the walls in Bodeguita, Havana.
In the bar of the expensive Nacional hotel, they mix a cocktail according to your wishes.
And those iconic cars from the 50’s have been driving around Havana so far, because once-imported jiguils and fiats in that salty and wet air have long since rotted.
Although Havana, especially its oldest part, is very dilapidated, there are not so many beautiful colonial monuments anywhere in the vicinity, as right there.
Also, other places are transformed into beauty, such as Trinidad.
Since tourism brings much-needed money, its development is green.
Over the past fifteen years, new hotels have grown not only in Havana, but especially on Varadero, on the coast of Holguín province and on islets such as Cayo Coco, Cayo Guillermo and others.
Kuba offers one more taste, which is unique in the world and is especially appreciated by trailers.
The cane from the large plantations was transported to the sugar mills on the narrow tracks of the steam and motor trains.
Their fleet has not been renewed for many decades, so there are functioning machines, whose contours are elsewhere only in museums.
Where to go?
Most of Cuba's most-known venues don't miss
And so there's not much need to write about Varader or Havana.
Perhaps just a reminder that there is a museum in Hemingway's already mentioned homestead and anyone who likes this writer should not miss the visit.
Viñales Valley One of the most picturesque corners of Cuba is the Viñales valley in the west of the island.
Lush vegetation grows from limestone bedrock.
Mogots, limestone homoles and rocks rise steeply from the bottom of the valley and fertile soil has taken hold in their cavities, thanks to which there are also weathered rocks covered with dense vegetation.
The valley is a place where perhaps the highest-quality Cuban tobacco is grown.
Its final form can be encountered if you visit one of the Havana factories for the production of cigars.
It may be interesting to someone that the Chilean work of Major Zeman was filmed there.
Trinidad
An ancient city that was founded not long after the Spaniards colonized the island.
Glory and wealth were brought to him by the slave and sugar trade.
Wealth has reincarnated into the beauty and exhibition of a small city.
The colonial Spanish architecture, significantly influenced by later French settlers, has hardly changed in four centuries.
Trinidad, between the sea and Escambray, is a place so remote that in the 50's of the last century there was not a road that would connect the county with the rest of the country.
The isolation helped him stay away.
Nothing was built there, and that's why nothing crashed, everyone was happy to have a roof over their heads.
If a person has more time for Trinidad than one in the afternoon, then it is also worth looking at interesting places in the vicinity, such as Playa Ancón beach or the Cukrovar Valley.
In the evening you can go to Casa de la Música, which comes to life after dark, and the music in it quiets over the morning.
Baracoa
A small town in the very east of Cuba is described as the most beautiful on the island.
Surely such an opinion influences the path itself.
Both roads that lead from Guantánamo must cross high and rugged mountains and pass through nature, which human activity has barely affected.
Thanks to its intact nature, the eastern outpost of Cuba has been placed on the World Heritage List, thanks to its intact
They make the best chocolate there, in the church they have the Columbus Cross (in fact it originated about a hundred years after Columbus's voyage), and downhill, where stands the former castle and today's hotel El Castillo, you can see several table mountains, the most famous of which is El Yunque.
Coffee, rum, cigars, the Sierra Maestra Mountains, the Bay of Pigs and the island of Isla de Pinos, the Tropicana cabaret, the sanctuary of Cuban Patron Caridad del Cobre, the santeria, African religions professed by the descendants of former slaves could be described in many pages.
But, as they rightly say, see better once.
Allies in Afghanistan are passive, Americans say
Few soldiers and the lack of necessary equipment, according to the United States, affect the course of the mission in Afghanistan.
According to Defense Secretary Robert Gates, NATO is expected to outline a concrete plan for the next few years.
The mission in Afghanistan is not going according to expectations, the representatives of countries that have troops in the southern part of the country agreed in Edinburgh, Scotland.
In addition to increasing opposition from the Taliban movement, eight participating states are also discussing further plans and the coordination of allies.
While the United States this week, through Defense Secretary Robert Gates, urged NATO troops for greater activity, the UK sees a priority in strengthening the Afghan government's authority and intensifying development aid.
"Development in Afghanistan is tangible, but military force can only be one part of a solution.
The next is to allow residents themselves to kick-start the further development of the economy and security," British Defence Secretary Des Browne told the BBC.
Ninety percent
According to U.S. military figures, Afghanistan's activities are the highest in five years, according to U.S. military data.
The number of violent actions increased by 27 percent compared to last year, in the southern province of Helmand by 60 percent.
NATO countries have about 40,000 troops in Afghanistan, of which about a quarter in southern territory.
According to the United States, however, the Allies came to the very limit in the organization and equipment of the troops.
Specifically, they ask for additional helicopters, soldiers as well as experts for training Afghan troops.
"We're going to have to think seriously about concrete goals for the next time and ways to achieve them," Gates said.
In a similar sense, NATO Secretary-General Jaap de Hoop also spoke today.
"I'm not happy with what we've got on the land and air in Afghanistan.
We have about ninety percent of the troops and equipment we would need, and that's not enough," De Hoop told Reuters at the conclusion of three-day talks with the Japanese government in Tokyo.
Who's Passive
There have also been controversies among the participating countries in recent months over whether all allies do their best in Afghanistan.
Germany, for example, has been criticized for passivity.
"The Germans have quite a number of representations in Afghanistan.
But they are deployed in areas where there is minimal violence and fighting demands on them are not as high as that of us in the southern and eastern parts of the country," Gates said.
This week, the British tabloid Sun described German soldiers as lazies.
"Afghan bumpers exposed: The Germans play while our boys fight," the Sun wrote in the material, accompanied by images of German soldiers playing table tennis.
Germany's Bild switched to the counter-attack and imprinted a picture of the British unit during a rugby fight in the southern part of Afghanistan.
The national debt rises to a trillion in 2009
For every Czech there is a national debt of more than 84,000 crowns.
The total debt rose to 842.7 billion crowns at the end of September.
The government planned to borrow 159.2 billion crowns this year.
At the beginning of December, however, the Ministry lowered the plan to 152.8 billion crowns thanks to the better development of state budget management.
At the end of September, the national debt of the Czech Republic rose to 842.7 billion crowns.
In the nine months of this year it increased by 40.2 billion crowns.
Since June, however, it has decreased by 13.4 billion crowns.
Today, the Ministry of Finance confirmed its data on the national debt from the beginning of October.
For every Czech there is a debt of more than 84,000 crowns.
In the whole of last year, the state became more than 111 billion crowns in debt.
"For the time being, it seems that, thanks to a lower deficit for next year, we could only surpass the trillion-crown limit in 2009.
Next year the debt should be between 900 billion and a trillion crowns," Patria Finance analyst David Marek told news server iHNed.cz.
Vladimír Pikora, an analyst at Next Finance, sees it similarly: "We could get to exceed one trillion during 2009.
When exactly will it depend on the next budget we don't know yet.
If it turned out that the situation on the political scene would change, politicians would be willing to save more, it could shift to 2010.
But I don't anticipate that change," he stated.
Debt is rising
The national debt consists of the debts of the central government and arises primarily by the accumulation of state budget deficits.
The national debt is financed by Treasury vouchers, government bonds, direct loans or, for example, loans from the European Investment Bank.
The state's internal indebtedness has risen by 38.5 billion to 719.4 billion crowns so far this year.
The state's foreign debt has risen by 1.7 billion crowns to 123.3 billion crowns so far this year.
At the end of September, the so-called tradable national debt amounted to 803.3 billion crowns and has fallen by 13.7 billion crowns since June.
The average maturity of the national debt has risen to 6.5 years.
"Total debt rose 13 percent year-on-year for the first to third quarter, which is really a lot for how the economy is doing.
Interestingly, the increase is generated mainly for bonds, where there is an increase of 20 percent.
It's bad that the debt is rising, but on the other hand, it shows that there will be something to trade with when there is pension reform," Pikora commented.
Better than expected
The strategy for the financing and management of the national debt for 2007 shows that the government planned to borrow 159.2 billion crowns this year.
At the beginning of December, however, the Ministry lowered the plan to 152.8 billion crowns thanks to the better development of state budget management.
The amount consists mainly of the deficit of the state budget, which will probably reach 76 billion crowns this year instead of the projected 91.3 billion crowns.
Furthermore, these are the planned maximum resources for the Czech Consolidation Agency 13.3 billion crowns, a ten-billion-dollar provision for the payment of pensions, the financing of government bonds for 43 billion crowns and loan repayments from the European Investment Bank amounting to 1.8 billion crowns.
The woman is due to appear in court in Khartoum today for giving her name to a teddy bear
A British teacher tried in Sudan for insulting Islam was sentenced to a 15-day imprisonment by a Khartoum tribunal on Thursday night and deported, according to her lawyer.
The British woman has been accused of allowing six to seven-year-old pupils to give a teddy bear the name Mohammed despite any depiction of the prophet Islam being banned.
She was punished with a whiplash.
Britain's forty-four-year-old teacher, working at Unity High School in Khartooum, Sudain, was arrested at her home on Sunday and accused of insulting Islam and rebellion, in accordance with Article 125 of the Sudanese Penal Code, after the parents of several pupils filed a complaint with the Sudanese Ministry of Education.
In fact, our story begins as early as the month of September, when the teacher in question asks his six to seven-year-old pupils to choose a name for a class teddy bear.
“Eight names will be heard from the mouths of the children, among them Hasan, Abdullah and Mohammed,” said Robert Boulos, the school’s principal.
After a vote takes place in the classroom, 20 of the 23 children will eventually choose Mohammed as the most popular name.
Of course, Muhammad is the name of a Muslim prophet, but above all it is a widespread first name in Sudan.
The children then created a book for their class with a photo of a teddy bear accompanied by the caption: "My name is Muhammad".
The bear himself did not have a label with this name, but the problem is in the depiction of the prophet himself, considered an insult to Islam.
One of Gillian Gibbons, who teaches at the same school in Sudan, has specified that he knows her well and that she would never do anything that could be offensive to any religious belief: "I only regret asking the children to vote for a name," he said, is merely "innocent misconduct."
Such an opinion is shared by many teachers who describe it as "very attentive to their pupils" and add that "never talk about religion in the classroom".
Recall that the first name Muhammad is also the second most commonly granted first name in the United Kingdom, written in English in fourteen different ways.
This first name, often chosen by Muslim families, represents a way to honor a prophet (often awarded to the firstborn).
But what are these rules regarding the use of this baptismal name?
Is it possible, for example, to give them a toy?
Of course, it's a matter of interpretation.
In the last round of negotiations this Wednesday in Vienna, they failed to reach an agreement on the provincial's future status.
In the end, failure.
Serbs and Kosovo Albanians ended their sixth and final round of talks in Baden, Austria, on Wednesday morning, without being able to overcome divergent views on the future status of this separatist province.
“It is regrettable that both sides have not been able to reach a status agreement,” European negotiator Wolfgang Ischinger said during a press conference in Vienna.
His U.S. counterpart, Frank Wisner, emphasized that peace in the area is "at stake" and has appealed to Belgrade and Pristina to heed their commitments to peace and be ready for dialogue even after the negotiations.
“We don’t expect violence anytime soon, but we’re entering a challenging period,” he added, adding that “it’s obvious that the situation is tense.”
Negotiation ends on December 10
Negotiators of the Troika, made up of representatives of the European Union, the United States and Russia, will travel to Kosovo for the last time on Monday.
The future of the province, where 90 percent are Albanian-speaking populations, could turn out in a clearer light as of December 10, when Ischinger, Wisner and Russian negotiator Alexander Bocan-Kharchenko are due to submit their report to the U.N. Security Council.
Talks in Baden, near Vienna, represent the sixth and final round of negotiations since the U.N
Americans and Europeans are counting on the talks to end on December 10.
The Russians, who have previously blocked Kosovo's independence in the U.N. Security Council, by contrast, are insisting for negotiations to continue beyond that deadline.
"Russia has been and remains inclined to a solution negotiated, a compromise solution," Bocan-Kharchenko said.
Unilateral declaration of independence?
After eight years of managing the international community, the West regards Kosovo's independence under the supervision of the EU as the only viable solution.
Pristina declares that in the event that the Security Council does not decide in its favour, it is prepared to declare independence unilaterally.
Belgrade insists on widespread autonomy.
"Unfortunately, we did not come to any agreement," announced the president of the Kosovo self-government Fatmir Sejdiu in a Baden hotel, where this "last chance negotiations" took place since Monday.
Hashim Thaçi, a former rebel commander of Kosovo's Albanians and the province's future prime minister, expressed regret.
He added, however, that the autonomy plan presented by Belgrade "belongs to the past that led to murders and deportations."
"Domino effect"
Serbian President Boris Tadić, on the other hand, believes that a compromise is still under consideration and has warned the Albanian majority in Kosovo about the consequences of the province's possible unilaterally proclaimed independence.
Belgrade is particularly concerned about the "dominous effect" in an area that is still unstable as a result of wars for independence in the 1990s.
“It doesn’t mean for Serbia, though,” he said, “that compromise is impossible.”
On Tuesday, the Serbian delegation insisted that all decisions belong exclusively to the Security Council, in which its Russian ally has veto power.
"The Kosovo parliament will have the final say"
"Only the solution arising from the Security Council is acceptable for Serbia," Serbian Prime Minister Vojislav Koštunica said.
"These negotiations lasted two years.
It has to end as it has begun: on the floor of the Security Council."
Fatmir Sedjiu told reporters that "the Kosovo parliament will have the last word on status," adding that if there was a unilateral declaration of independence by the Albanian-speaking population, it would take place "in agreement" with the West.
Bush has reached a deal on Israelis and Palestinians
At the opening of an international peace conference in Annapolis in Maryland convened at his initiative, U.S. President George Bush announced that Israelis and Palestinians had finally agreed on a joint statement.
He said Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Olmert and Palestinian President Mahmud Abbas had agreed to begin talks immediately with the intention of resolving a 60-year-long dispute between the two nations once and for all.
In the presence of both men, Bush has vowed to do everything he can to help them conclude a "peace treaty" before the end of 2008.
In the meantime, Palestinians must destroy "terrorist infrastructures" and Israel must put an end to colonial activities, Bush said, recalling the responsibilities for both sides from the "travel map" to the peace they adopted in 2003.
The head of the White House emphasized that negotiations between Israel and the Palestinian Authority led by Abbas aim at "the conclusion of a peace treaty that would resolve all undecided issues without exception, including central issues."
This statement includes three issues at the heart of the dispute between Israelis and Palestinians: the future borders of Palestine, the status of Jerusalem, and the fate of Arab refugees expelled in the 1948 conflict from their homes on the territory of today's state of Israel.
Abbas took the floor after the U.S. president and emphasized that subsequent negotiations should be "total and thorough" and to touch all aspects of the solution, mentioning "Jerusalem, refugees, borders, colonies, water, security, among other things."
Olmert then spoke at the tribune, expressing that subsequent negotiations should be "direct and continuous" and that "it is necessary to ensure that it is concluded during 2008".
Bush determined the end of 2008 as the date of implementation of his "vision of two states", Palestinian and Israeli, existing side-by-side.
Saudi Arabia revealed that it had made a commitment from Bushem in this sense even before deciding to participate in the conference.
Saudi Arabia, whose participation in Annapolis is a success for Washington, initiated a proposal adopted by all Arab countries, which offers Israel peace in exchange for abandoning all Arab territories seized during the 1967 war, including East Jerusalem.
Annapolis means "a crossroads in the history of the Middle East mainly because it fights against the roots of the conflict and its central aspects," Prince Saud said in an interview with an Arabic-speaking diary shielded by Saudi capital.
According to the prosecutor, the officers aren't responsible.
Exactly what happened on Sunday?
As is always the case with such small messages from the Black Chronicle, the official versions do not coincide with what is said among people.
What are the circumstances of the accident?
According to the first results of the investigation of the General Inspection of the French State Police (Inspection générale de la police nationale, IGPN), a police car drove through Louise-Michel Street in the village of Villiers-le-Bel on Sunday shortly after 17 o'clock.
“The vehicle wasn’t in intervention mode, but it was on patrol and passed at a speed of 40 to 50 km/h, it didn ́t have sidelights turned on,” explains the police source.
The green Kawasaki motorcycle, "unregistered, and therefore without permission to ride on public roads" (read below), driven by two youths without a helmet, drove through "very high speed".
"The bike crashed from the left side into a police car after he didn't give preference from the right," one police officer clarified.
A test of promile alcohol in the blood carried out on the crew of the vehicle turned out to be negative.
The condition of the car (broken ancestor, fragmented windshield, broken bumpers) testifies to the sharpness of the impact.
Marie-Thérèse de Givry, the Prosecutor of the Republic of Pontoise (République de Pontoise), reacted very quickly, and announced yesterday morning that the IGPN had tasked with investigating "acts of involuntary manslaughtering and failure to provide assistance to persons at risk," at the same time taking charge of dûreté publique du Val
On the occasion of an afternoon press conference, the prosecutor said an IGPN-led investigation excluded, at this stage, the responsibility of police officers for the accident.
The three witnesses to the accident, who were interrogated on Sunday evening, confirmed "the version of the police, namely that the mini-motorbike approached them relatively quickly from the left".
A groove, no doubt caused by the friction of the clips to the ground, showed yesterday that both vehicles remained wedged until when the police car was able to brake.
Vehicles have been secured and will undergo technical expertise.
An autopsy was carried out on the bodies of the two victims yesterday.
What's what happened right after?
Speakings spread quickly at Villiers-le-Bel last day that officers were supposed to leave the crime scene quickly without providing first aid to two youths.
IGPN, in all caution, was also supposed to exclude "any serious offense" at this point.
"It's a more challenging aspect of an investigation that requires more searches and that needs to be very careful," the police said.
"Libération" met an eyewitness of those few moments that followed the accident.
One of his windows runs right where the accident took place.
"Immediately, together with our cousin, who is a nurse at the hospital, we ran downstairs to try to resurrect them," the witness said.
We couldn't do anything."
He then saw "a limping, completely frightened policeman.
Two others were trying to resurrect one of the youngsters, and the founder spoke on the phone."
In the face of the rapidly growing tension and certainty of those present that the situation of the youths was hopeless, the officers were taken away by the car of colleagues, who in the meantime arrived at the scene.
According to the same witness, the ambulance arrived "about twenty minutes after the departure of the officers."
They also performed a heart massage, but in vain.
"Don't let anyone say that the police authorities didn't come to the youth's aid.
These youths were given first aid.
Firefighters arrived immediately.
Everything has been done to save them," the prosecutor pointed out.
Tensions are rising in Pakistan
Pakistani police on Monday suppressed the first demonstrations since declaring a state of emergency by General Musharraf, two days earlier.
In the city of Lahor, in the east of the country, about 2000 lawyers demonstrating against measures taken as part of a state of emergency have been wounded by grenades with tear gas that, according to witnesses, have been used by riot forces.
The latter responded with stones, injuring several police officers.
At least 350 lawyers have been summoned.
Similar scenarios as in Karachi and Rawalpindi, where officers attacked and wounded lawyers.
Several should have been summoned.
In Islamabad, Supreme Court advocates called for a general strike and announced demonstrations, as a result of a wave of about five hundred arrests of opponents, human rights fighters and lawyers over the weekend.
Access streets to the Supreme Court were dammed by roadblocks on Monday morning, in order to prevent gatherings.
Other demonstrations were scheduled to take place in Multan (centre) and in Peshaware (Northwest).
On Monday, a movement of lawyers won the support of religious parties grouped in Muttahida Majlis-e-Amal, whose president "urged the population to send flowers to judges who opposed the state of emergency, and garbage to those who took an oath before Musharraf."
"Musharraff's promise doesn't apply"
Pakistan's Supreme Court is the direct target of an emergency measure announced Saturday evening.
Over the next few days, he was due to comment on the legality of the re-election of President Musharraf in an indirect presidential election that took place on October 6.
The court's president, Iftikhar Mohammad Chaudhry, has been removed and replaced.
“Everything we are witnessing is illegal, unconstitutional and against judgments of the Supreme Court,” he said.
The promise of Parvíz Musharraf to give up the leadership of the army and become a civilian president "does not apply "after the introduction of the state of emergency, Deputy Information Minister Tariq Azeem said on Monday.
He was expected to give up his military uniform by November 15, when his first presidential term ended.
The deputy information minister also confirmed that the January elections could be postponed.
Otherwise, the Pakistani government also refuted speculation in the country, according to which the deputy head of the army was supposed to ordain President Musharraf under house arrest.
More than a third of pregnancies still make up an unexpected pregnancy
This calculation includes unwanted pregnancy (24%), as well as poorly planned or occurring in an unexpected way.
More than one pregnancy out of three (36 percent) is still unexpected today, according to a study led by the French National d'études demographiques (Ined), the State Institute of Demographic Studies, forty years after the adoption of the so-called Neuwirth Act, which enacted contraception in France.
This calculation includes unwanted pregnancy (24%), as well as poorly planned or occurring in an unexpected way.
Various methods of contraception, such as hormonal contraceptives or sterilization, still show 5% to 6% failure rate, Ined estimates.
Enacting contraceptives has enabled better "fertility control," the institute argues, noting that the rate of use of hormonal contraceptives in women between 20 and 44 years of age has risen from 5% in 1970 today to 60%.
Finally, eight births out of ten is well planned today.
One couple in five have a birth even calculated for a month accurately and preferably in the spring, which is considered the most enjoyable time of year for the birth of a child.
Remarkable repair of the space station
More than seven hours of work in space was needed by astronaut Scott Parazynski and members of his crew from the International Space Station (ISS) to make a remarkable correction of the torn solar panel, necessary for the station's further scientific activity.
It was a risky ascent into space, which collected records in terms of duration, the distance from residential modules, the speed with which it was planned, and the rates of "diligence" that astronauts expended to repair.
Difficulties surfaced on Tuesday, while astronauts from Discovery unloaded the Harmony module, a large cylindrical unit designed to install a European and Japanese scientific laboratory (see frame).
This module is a kind of lander for module-laboratories.
However, they must be supplied with electricity.
Therefore, the second task of the crew was to relocate a seventeen-and-a-half-tone module producing electricity using solar panels, which was already located on the ISS, so that it anchored at its final habitat.
After relocating it, the crew began decomposing the module's sixty-seven-foot solar panels.
And in this, one of the panels, decomposed as a tug-of-warn harmonica, as a result of pinching the cable, was damaged on an area of almost one meter.
Decomposition has been suspended to prevent further panel damage.
The astronauts, who were originally supposed to carry out a cheaper exit to the Discovery ship, and then go repair another solar panel, whose rotation system was malfunctioning, were forced to devote themselves to the damaged panel.
And it was Scont Parazynski who was chosen to go flying to the end of the robotic arm of the ship, added to the station's shoulder, and make repairs.
He's one of the best "spacewalkers" (spacewalker) of the U.S. Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA).
Tall, robust, very agile, has a very well-crafted chest and long arms.
At the age of 46, this original profession was commissioned by a doctor to "operate" an "injured" solar panel.
After examining the damages in detail, he cut off the faulty cable and fastened a kind of "splint" to the damaged areas.
He was assisted by a second "spacewalker" that remained close to the leveling chamber, and also from within, by the other astronauts, among them Peggy Witson, the crew commander.
The tools he used, a mini-hockeyder covered with an insulating agent to prevent electroshock from being electroshocked on several hundred volt-producing solar panels, were invented and "handy" produced by astronauts aboard the station.
At the end of the operation, Scott Parazynski waited until the panel was completely decomposed and then inventarized his tools and returned to the inside of the station.
Yesterday afternoon, astronauts enjoyed a half-day rest before today, when seven astronauts are returning to the Discovery ship to return to Earth on Wednesday as planned.
EADS shares weaken
EADS shares are losing noticeably on the Paris Stock Exchange, after the company operating in the aerospace and defense industry announced that it will have to spend 1.2 to 1.4 billion euros to face the delay of the A400M.
EADS lost 4% and is now at 21.96 euros on the Paris Stock Exchange.
The financial group was forced to re-evaluate its projected production results for 2007 as a result of delays resulting from the slowly progressing forward development of the A400M engine, which is a condition of initiating flight.
The cost of mitigating the delays in the delivery of the military cargo aircraft, announced in October, was quantified at "1.2 to 1.4 billion euros, of which more than a billion for Airbus," the parent company of European designer EADS announced.
Due to these costs, EADS will be forced to announce a new profit outlook before interest and taxes (EBIT) is presented next Thursday when presenting its quarterly results.
The group also revealed that a new cost estimate corresponds to a six-month delay.
Recall that EADS production results fell 86% in 2006 mainly due to the costs associated with the delay of another program, the giant A380.
Initially, the group anticipated that it would provide the first delivery of the A400M to the French army in October 2009.
Judgment for the status quo
Sunday's elections will take place without tense expectations: the Russians will mostly elect the party of Putin, United Russia.
Confirminating, after the end of the very few free election campaigns, their tension towards stability, and even authoritarianism.
Today, three days before elections to the Chamber of Deputies, Russian President Vladimir Putin will tell voters on public broadcasters a pre-recorded television message, the contents of which, however, are kept secret.
He does so as the leader of United Russia, the pro-Kremlin party, which seems to celebrate a crushing victory.
Yesterday, when he was accepting the ambassadors of foreign states, the president did not let anything come out of him and was satisfied with another attack on the West accused of meddling - his favorite theme during the campaign.
"We know the price of true democracy and we wish an honest, most transparent and open election possible, an election without the slightest flaw in beauty," he added.
In the eyes of the opposition, this is far from the case.
They condemn the campaign marked by various coercions.
Communist leader Gennady Zyuganov says that “there have never been such dirty elections, even under Yeltsin.”
After repressions accompanied by demonstrations last weekend, former chess chess champion Garry Kasparov was sentenced to five days in prison for an illegal demonstration, according to his lawyer, she continues to be banned from visiting.
"To the Russians, the mentality of homo sovietica remained"
In the background of these parliamentary elections of simple tense anticipation, there are two more open-minded questions:
- "Who will become Putin's successor in the presidency?
The official newspaper yesterday published the date of the first round of the presidential election, March 2, 2008; candidates can introduce themselves to the public by December 23.
Vladimir Putin, elected in 2000 and re-elected in 2004, cannot seek a third mandate in a row.
However, no follower emerges.
- "What role will Putin play after March?"
Will he be prime minister?
Or the head of a parliamentary majority and an all-powerful United Russia party that chooses a government and a president?
Either way, the outgoing ruling power will prevail all along the lines in the parliamentary elections, as well as in the presidential elections.
It's a tradition.
"To the Russians, the mentality of homo sovietica has remained, they do not feel masters of their destiny," believes sociologist Lev Gudkov, director of the independent Levada centre.
The historian Nikolai Kopossov of the State University of Sankt-Peterburg observes a more distant context: civil society has picked up, in this country Christianized five centuries after Europe; that glorious common spirit has delayed the formation of individualism.
“Putin continued the paternalistic model of the state, perfectly accepted by the population, all the more so because this new paternalism was accompanied not by terror, but by certain economic well-being,” said Maria Lipman of the Carnegie Foundation.
Today, the Russians "voluntarily give up personal responsibility", which they briefly demonstrated after the collapse of the USSR.
"Why think?"
This phenomenon affects all social classes, including the business world.
Even the official elite intends to maintain the status quo: 65 governors (out of a total of 85 heads of the Russian regions) are managing the local candidates of the United Russia.
New freedoms taken from the West are often synonymous with chaos, as in the 1990s.
Public opinion prefers stability.
Also, in the name of stability, the Russians will not elect extremists.
The conclusion of Tamara Cherednichenko, a fifty-four-year-old professor: "What to think about?
“Those in power are not so bad, so why change them?”
Burdened memory
After more than sixty years in a confidential regime, the Nazi archives from Bad Arolsen, which recount the fates of 17.5-million victims of Hitler's regime, are opening up to the public.
47 million archival documents deposited 27 km in Bad Arolsen (Hesse) until now have been accessible only for "humanitarian" purposes, i.e. individually and nominally, at the request of a former victim or their authorized persons.
Eleven archives-intensed states - including Germany, France, Israel, and the United States - agreed in May to open them to scientists and historians.
Since the process of ratification of this agreement by all states is over, the opening took effect yesterday.
Sensitive Information
The free access of historians to Bad Arolsen will make it possible to examine "particularly the transfer of prisoners, the population of camps and the state of health of forcibly workers," explains Reto Meistern, director of these archives managed by the International Committee of the Red Cross (Comité international de la Croix-rouge, CICR).
Until recently, several states, among them Germany, were against making these nominal documents available to the public, due to the sensitive nature of some information.
What are the presumed homosexuality or mental disabilities of some victims of deportation.
Registers of concentration camps or arrest warrants compiled by the Gestapo were assembled after the war for the practical purpose of finding deported or forced labor subjected to civilian citizens, bringing them proof of their internment in order to open the way to their compensation.
Even today, more than 300 permanent employees in Bad Arolsen are engaged in tens of thousands of individual applications.
However, for part of the scientific community, access to Bad Arolsen represents only a limited contribution.
"Nothing fundamentally new can be expected from these documents," Prof. Wolfgang Benz, director of the Centre for the Study of Anti-Semitism of Berlin's Technische Universität, said in 2006.
The EU and India want to achieve a free trade treaty in 2008
Currently, bilateral trade between Europe and India reaches almost 60 billion euros a year.
The EU is India's first trading partner and accounts for 18% of its external trade.
The European Union and India are expected to sign a free trade treaty in 2008, European Trade Commissioner Peter Mandelson and his Indian counterpart Kamal Nath.
In April of last year, EU foreign ministers gave the green light to the European Commission to start negotiations with a view to signing free trade treaties (accords de libre échange, ALE) with India, South Korea and the Association of Southeast Asian States (Asean). Bilateral trade between Europe and India is already reaching almost 60 billion euros a year.
The EU is India's first trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
The EU is also its first foreign investor, but India, according to the European Commission, is the EU's ninth trading partner.
"The free trade agreement dominates both economies tremendously" and "India gets better access to the 450-millione-thriving European market," Peter Mandelson said.
Europe represents the first or second market for each of the top ten products exported by India, the European commissioner clarified. The Asian and European giant exchange "47 billion euros in goods" and "ten billion euros in services" each year, Danièle Smadja, the head of the European Commission's delegation in New Delhi this week, regretting that bilateral trade "has not yet reached its full potential".
(Source OF AFP)
The Bank of England took note of concern and offered £10bn in a five-week auction
The Bank of England on Thursday signaled that it intends to alleviate current fears that conditions in the credit market will deteriorate again, announcing an offer of 10-billion pounds at a five-week auction at a base interest rate of 5.75%, an offer valid for an unusually long period.
"In order to ease the concerns that financial market conditions will be very unfavourable at the end of the year and to give banks greater certainty in the way in which to secure their cash position for this period, the Bank intends to offer a significant part of its planned reserve during the maintenance period, which will begin on 6 December in the form of a five-week operation on the open market," she said.
The bank specifies that 10 billion pounds (14 billion euros) will thus be placed on the financial market on December 6 at 12.15 GMT until January 10, at the basic interest rate.
It seems to be the first time BOE is pumping cash into the financial market in a five-week open market operation.
They normally last a week.
The BOE adds in its communiqué that it "remains ready to implement additional measures to maintain interest rates on a daily basis" of interbank loans at the same level at its base rate.
The inter-day interbank interest rate was at 5.829% on Wednesday.
The three-month interest rate settled at 6.6%, the highest number since September 18.
From the beginning of the summer financial crisis, sums placed on the financial market by the Bank of England were associated with fee rates, which discouraged banks from taking advantage of this offer.
According to some speculations reinforced by the evolution of the pound's exchange rate against the euro, many would rather opt for sums placed on the financial market by the European Central Bank with lower rates and more guaranteed discretion.
UMP (Union pour un mouvement populaire) and NC (Nouveau Centre) are refusing to deal with the proposals of the Socialists (PS) on purchasing capacity
UMP and Nouveau Centre clubs declined on Thursday to discuss individual paragraphs of the socialist bullage bill, which amounts to rejecting the text.
On the agenda, the text of the bill came within the framework of the so-called socialist "nest" (a meeting initiated by an individual parliamentary club) and included dozens of measures adopted on Tuesday by the PS presidency, among which an increase in the employment premium by 50%, the resumption of fluctuating TIPPs, traffic cheques or rent regulation.
Since UMP and NC voted against the negotiations of individual paragraphs (180 votes against 115), the session was concluded after a general debate, which became an opportunity for an opinion exchange, sometimes more vivid, between a majority and the opposition, a few hours before Nicolas Sarkozy's televised appearance on purchasing capacity.
The head of PS Jean-Marc Ayrault has announced that his club will renament these proposals in their entirety in the form of amendments to an amendment to the Financy Bill, which will be debated by the House next week.
A new festival is born
As a result of much speculation that C3 Presents is setting up a new festival in Philadelphia, this promoter/producer of the Vineland Festival in New Jersey, in collaboration with the Festival of the Republic by British organizer Melvino Benn.
“It’s an honor for our company to work with a man who inspired the current festival movement in America without exaggeration,” revealed Charlie Jones of C3 to Billboard.com.
The C3 is produced by the Chicago Lollapalooza Festival and the Austin City Limits Music Festival.
Vineland in the state of New Jersey is the summer's destination for campers.
The festival is expected to take place from August 8 to 10 on a 500-acre private farm, about 40 minutes from Philadelphia.
The director of the Republic festival, formerly known as Mean Fiddler, is one of the world's most acknowledged festival organizers.
In 1989, he took the helm of the Reading Festival in the United Kingdom, which attracts 80,000 people every year.
In 1999, Melvin Benn added Leeds Festival to his portfolio. In 2006, the Republic Festival founded the Latitude Festival in Henham Park in Suffolk.
Starting in 2002, Melvin Benn, as co-owner, is also in charge of production, licenses and organizing tasks at the Glastonbury Festival, which today has a capacity of up to 177,500 people.
Charlie Jones of C3 describes Glastonbury as "the most inspiring event he's ever experienced," saying his firm and Melvina Benna's firm are working concurrently to give the region a European-style festival event.
“Originally we chose Philadelphia as a market, and together with the Fairmount Parks Commission, we worked hard together to start a festival in downtown Philadelphia.”
“We negotiated with the city and the Commission was motivated and enthusiastic about the project.
Unfortunately, due to the current leadership of the city, we have become bogged down into politics, adds Charlie Jones.
"Luckily, we've had the opportunity to discuss with Melvin Benn and we're going to establish a great festival in an exceptional venue that will ultimately serve Philadelphia residents."
C3 Presents resurrected Lollapalooza in Chicago's Grant Park in 2005, after excelling on the festival stage in 2002 by organizing the Austin City Limits Music Festival.
The gross sales of both events for 2007 amounted to 9.8M$ and 11,3M$.
Both of these festivals take place in an urban environment.
“Melvin Benn’s European festival model takes place in the countryside, while our festivals take place more on green spaces in cities,” says Charlie Jones.
Our goal is to reconcile both concepts in a certain way so as to create both the impression of a rural festival in the European and urban after the Americas."
Vineland is located roughly halfway between Philadelphia and Atlantic City.
“The municipality of Vineland, including the mayor, enthusiastically supports the realisation of this event,” says Charlie Jones.
The latter refuses to divulge the terms of the agreement, but argues that "they are sufficiently advantageous to allow them to invest in such an event and to welcome a festival that will, hopefully, have a long duration."
Charlie Jones says the capacity of this festival is "very high," similar to the capacity of the Bonnaroo Music Festival in Tennessee, which accommodates 80,000 people.
He says invitations have already been sent to groups, but nothing can yet be confirmed when the program is yet to be put to print.
“Vineland will have a rocky touch, but the music will be varied so that we can attract a wide range of viewers if possible,” says Carlie Jones.
The partners of C3, who received the Top Independent Promoter at the final Billboard Touring Awards, are Charlie Walker and Charles Attal.
Montrey Faces Terminator
It's not easy to attract Hollywood movie studios to Montreal when the Terminator himself opposes it...
This persuasive mission is one of the delegate's tasks.
His mandate, however, is "politically challenging" because California governor Arnold Schwarzenegger condemns "runaway productions."
It's difficult to fall "Governator" into the claws??
"We have to be careful, it's the industry that decides where to go.
If he finds the conditions in Montreal favorable, he'll go there.
Our role is to offer those that they will consider to be the best,” he said.
Pursuition for festivals
For the moment, however, Denise Turcotte's priority is the promotion of the local film.
It's already beginened the hunt for the Oscars.
At all West Coast festivals, whether it's festivals in Sundance, Denver, Seattle or San Francisco, it's going to be a matter of introducing quebek films, drawing attention to them and ensuring that directors give interviews.
The age of darkness had good publicity a few weeks ago at the 30th annual Mill Valley Film Festival.
It will be unveiled at the Palm Springs International Festival in January.
Shake hands with the Devil is on offer in Santa Monica and will be unveiled in November at the 30th Starz Denver Film Festival.
This month, Québec also unveiled four short and four feature films at the American Film Institute Fest.
Will there be a marathon at the end of the year on the stock exchange? Experts divided by 'subprime' crisis
Less than five weeks remain until the end of the administrative year, and investors are already with me in anticipation that there will be a traditional marathon at the end of the year.
This year, however, experts are not so sure that it will happen.
Banif believes that "from a statistical point of view, the month of December on the stock exchange has a positive course".
However, it is not appropriate to make predictions solely on the basis of statistics, in particular, because, in the current context, markets have, at least in the short term, some doubt."
These experts do not foresee that the stock exchange will move in the last weeks of the year, stating that "the exchange market maintains the existence of liquidity, which is prepared to invest due to the lack of alternatives with guarantees, especially fixed income, influenced by the effect of turnover to quality securities, both in long and short sections".
Therefore, they believe that the stock exchange will have a rather calm course, not without volatility, "mainly because there is no known the impact of a probable fall in Fed (Federal reserve system) rates, due to the high level of oil prices and the dangers of stimulus for inflationary pressures".
Javier Barrio, head of BPI's sales in Spain, agrees that it's not the right time for bold statements that there's going to be a marathon.
"There's not much reason to rejoice," assures this expert.
Ibex lost nearly 4% of the maximum values recorded on November 8, when the index closed at 15 945.7 points.
However, not all experts have such a negative opinion.
Juan Solana, an Intermoney analyst, believes that Ibex 35 could end a year a little closer to 16,000 points by regrouping portfolios and traditional retirement deposits that occur annually at this time.
However, this expert suggests that everything will be a little different this year, as he believes that the marathon will take place earlier, in the second week of December after a long weekend on the occasion of Constitute Day.
This analyst does not believe that volatility would affect the direction of the market.
At the same time, Federico Olucha, an analyst at Inversis Banco, estimates that we are approaching technical levels and "therefore, we could observe the rebounce of the index, triggered by reports from the U.S
Analysts believe these consequences will be positive.
Analysts coincide in the selection of titles, which will take care of bringing Ibex to new maximum dimensions.
Solana of Intermoney confirms that "this year's turn for strong titles."
This expert would include both large banks and some construction companies in the portfolio, "because when this period comes, the energy sector will be very expensive."
Federico Olucha of Inversis Banco, by contrast, assures that the rises will go hand in hand with banking and the industrial sector.
Construction companies will also be able to enrich themselves on this marathon if it happens.
"ACS has been regaining positions for several months and we hope that other construction companies, such as Ferrovial and FCC, will follow the same path," explains the expert.
Cyclical consumption will be at the foreground in the last part of the year thanks to an increase in sales during the Christmas sale period.
This expert, on the other hand, believes that also technology will do very well, even if they are not very represented in the Spanish market in this field.
Five times in seven years
For the past seven years, December has only been a month of slumps on two occasions.
The real marathon at the end of the year took place in 2003, in an administrative year, when stock markets ended a crisis triggered by the collapse of a technological bubble.
This year, when Ibex 35 regained 28.17%, December recorded an increase of 6.68%.
We'll see if 2007 will be the fifth profitable year (Ibex has risen by 8.36% since January) and whether it manages to have its marathon at the end of the year.
Two hundred Branches of Bank Popular will be opened 12 hours from Friday to Saturday
Banka Popular decided to modernize most of its branches.
A total of two hundred, located in the most commercial parts of Spain.
These branches, operated under the Popular Max brand, are a big change for the bank.
Likewise for the other Spanish entities, because it is the first group that has decided to make such a jump and bet on the continuous opening hours of its branches until 20.30 hours and open on Saturdays from 10 a.m. to 20.30 p.m.
BBVA, another group, which has also repeatedly assured that it wants to extend the opening hours of its branches, has not yet decided on the move.
In the meantime, he intends to enter into an agreement with the unions to cancel the opening hours of 75% of their branches on Saturday in exchange for extending opening hours by one in the afternoon, although not for the public.
The results of this popularity experience will be crucial in ensuring that other actors join this initiative, which supports the opening hours of trades, or not.
This is precisely the goal of a project set out by the Bank of the Popular.
Obtain individual clients, who go to these places for their purchases, small and medium-sized companies, entrepreneurs and shops from selected streets, where branches will be opened.
The bank wants to open 200 of these branches throughout Spain within about 18 months, as this is not the opening of new branches, but only an adjustment to existing ones.
For this reason, the bank will modernize the branches it already has in business zones by adapting them to the profile of clients in individual streets.
Thus, the extension of the opening hours is complemented by a different conception of the space of the branch and a new, more up-to-date design in lighter colors.
Ángel Rivera, the bank's commercial director, assures that this restructuring will not involve a major investment, but greater recruitment, as it switches from six employees to a branch to eight to nine.
Branches will even be equipped with small and medium-sized meetings and will also sell non-financial products.
Positive experience for the group.
Banka Popular has already tested this initiative in three Madrido branches.
And it was a positive experience, as Ángel Rivera explains.
Branches in Madrid's Bravo Murillo, O'Donnell and Gran Vía streets were selected, and according to Rivero, it appears to have been a happy choice.
Banka Popular currently has 2,500 branches.
And the other 60 specialized immigrants, which are operated under the Mundocrid brand, also have very long opening hours and open even on Sundays and holidays.
PopularMax branches will not follow the BBVA model, where a number of its branches offer a large range of non-financial products for sale.
'We don't want to compete with our clients," Rivera says.
They've just agreed with Telefónica to sell cellphones through external staff.
Telecinco doubled the value of its rival Antena 3 for the first time on the stock market
We've done it'.
With this sentence, Paolo Vasile, Telecinco's executive chairman, expressed his satisfaction at the conclusion of trading, stating that the television he was directing had doubled the value of his rival Antena 3.
Only one thing spoiled his joy: 'what I'm sorry is that it didn't happen because we've risened more, but because we've fallen less," he said.
And he stressed that thanks to the weak market, 'we've fallen less.'
Telecinco ended trading yesterday with a shift of 2.54%.
This profit pushed their loss for the past part of the year to 15.66%.
According to Bloomberg data, the exchange value of this company controlled by Mediaset (with 52% capital) with Vocento (another 13%) is 4 488.88 million euros.
Its competitor Antena 3, which fell by 1.52% yesterday and declined 40.5% overall since January, has a half-value of 2 193.46 million.
The value of the other telecommunications firms that have been valued is 3.571 billion in the case of Sogecable, 2 518.53 million Prisa (publisher Cinco Días) and 1.779 billion Vocento.
According to Vasile, a comparison with Antena 3 is offered, because 'we're talking about two similar companies.
The relative fact is how much we are able to do in the same market, at what price and how we will evaluate it in the viewership'.
Telecinco announced last week that it was counting on surpassing a billion gross advertising revenues this year to become the first television channel, as assured by Giuseppe Tringali, executive chairman of Telecinco and Publiespaña, a branch that secures the group's advertising.
If predicted data were actually achieved, this TV channel would see a 3.6% increase in advertising turnover this year, compared to 965.4 million in 2006.
At the same time in the first nine months of the year, Antena 3's net turnover increased by 1.6% (and thus reached 734.4 million), while this television's net revenue increased by 0.7% (630.4 million).
One of the factors that analysts are focusing on, for the best relative development of Telecinco on the stock exchange, is the increased pay-out, which it has retained until now: in 2006, the company, chaired by Alejandro Echevarría, distributed 100% of its profits among its shareholders.
Vasile assures that even though it is early to decide, this coefficient could change after the purchase of 33% of Endemol together with its parent firm Mediaset.
'We have an official commitment to give at least 50%, although we've always announced a lot more.
This year I hope that we will be able to satisfy our shareholders, to whom we have already increased the value of 33% Endemol'.
According to analysts, the market is showing some fatigue at times.
Lehman Brothers recently lowered its Telecinco profit estimates in 2008, 4 percent in 2008 and 7% in 2009.
This is due to, explaining that the projected revenue growth has stalled from 0.5% to 8.4% in 2008 and from 5.5% to 5.1% in 2009.
The same firm reduced Antena 3's projected profit, at 4.5% in 2008 and 7.5% in 2009.
3 opinions
1.
Ibersecurities: recommends purchasing both titles primarily based on an increased return on dividend and long-term sustainable potential.
2.
Caja Madrid: rates Telecinco (accumulate) better than Antena 3 (maintain) because of its 'numerous advantages' and trade growth.
3.
JPMorgan: in both cases it recommends not overestimating them because they face 'similar threats' (an oversaturated market and a deteriorated economic environment).
The Congress has approved MiFID transposing in effect from November 1
In parallel with parliamentary hearings, which were concluded today by a vote of Congress on amendments incorporated by the Senate into the bill, the government has subjected a public hearing to a regulation that develops the new law, with the aim of bringing both into effect before the end of the year.
Representatives of individual parties equally highlighted the efforts of MPs and senators to negotiate and agree on the best standard in order to guarantee the protection of investors and the detailed regulation of the activities of entities necessary for the effective functioning of markets.
The MiFID Directive, which regulates the Securities Trading Act, foresees the modernisation of financial markets, while strengthening the protection of the investor, improves the supervisory powers of the State Securities Market Commission (CNMV) and promotes cooperation between the various supervisory authorities of the European Union.
This new regulation will make it possible to provide financial advisory services to both specialized companies and natural or legal persons and creates a new type of firms providing financial services (ESIS), financial advisory (AFI) companies that connect to securities companies, intermeding offices with securities and portfolio-managing companies.
In addition, the number of systems for the performance of operations with financial instruments is increased by connecting to regulated secondary markets multilateral trading systems and internalising order systems, which will compete with current stockbrokers.
In addition, the law obliges firms providing investment services to disseminate information in the market about key aspects of their business profile, exposure to risk, and ways to cope with risk, and requires conducting a statement of all operations carried out to simplify supervision by CNMV.
During the discussion in the Trade Commission of Congress, several adjustments were included, among which it stands out that securitized claims are considered a "complicated" financial instrument due to the high danger for the investor, which has been demonstrated by what occurred in the U.S. with high-risk subprime.
In addition, transposing the directive was used to lawfully set out functions that can be held by bachelors in economics and business management 30 years after a Royal Decree was approved in 1977 setting out professional functions reserved for economists.
At the same time, the Senate included the creation of new professions in the financial sector, such as an intermediary that works externally, will not require permission from CNMV, and will be able to provide goods trading services and acquiring clients for various investment firms at the same time.
The harmonised ISC increased by five-tenths to 4.1% in November
This indicator, which is later used by Eurostat to calculate the European Union's inflationary index, provides preliminary information on how prices developed in Spain during the month of November.
Its publication corresponds to the policies of Eurostat and the Central European Bank (CEB) to provide data in time and quality comparable to those achieved in the United States.
This preliminary indicator shall be calculated using the same process as is used for the Harmonised Index of Consumer Prices, but using different statistical modeling methods to estimate information that is not yet available at the time of publication.
Spain's INE (Stat State Institute for Statistics) recalled that the information provided by this provisional indicator is only indicative and therefore does not necessarily coincide with a final figure that comes into knowledge on 14 December this year.
"The ISC will continue to maintain at a relatively high level"
Second Deputy Prime Minister and Economy Minister Pedro Solbes said November's ISC increase to 4.1% was a "temporary number," but had also predicted that the price index would remain at a relatively "high level" in the coming months.
In any case, Solbes specified in the couloirs of Congress that "even if it won't be a favorable number," Spain's inflationary differential against the monetary union remains "more or less" the same over the past two years.
He also explained that November "was less surprising" than the previous month, when the government was not counting on such a high increase.
"We were expecting a value of around 4%," the economy minister said.
Virgin plans to make 42 billion euros with Northern Rock in three years
Since Northern Rock got into crisis in mid-September and had to resort to an emergency loan from the Bank of England, its deposits have plummeted by more than 50 per cent to 12 billion pounds (16.8 billion euros).
At the time, the bank managed record deposits worth more than 25 billion pounds, which gradually declined within two and a half months in disbelief of clients, despite the government offering a hundred percent guarantee for this money and extending it until the end of the crisis.
Virgin, which replaces the name of the entity with its Virgin Money brand, needs to receive around a quarter of the revenue invested in British banks over the next three years in order to earn the remaining 18 billion pounds, reaching a target of ££££»££££££££©
Richard Branson's group of tycoon, known in the United Kingdom with their striking advertising campaigns, plans to spend 50 million pounds (about £70 million) a year on advertising and marketing to achieve their resolution.
Northern Rock this week described Virgin's offer, made up by three other companies, as the one they would prefer to save them from bankruptcy.
