Bono has accepted an offer to stand at the head of the Toledonic PSOE nominee and, if they win, preside over Congress
The former defense minister, José Bono, has accepted an offer from Prime Minister and Socialist Secretary-General José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero to lead the PSOE (Spanish Workers' Socialist Party) candidate list in Toledo at the next general election and, in the event that the Socialists win a general election, become president of Congress.
This was announced at a press conference by PSOE Secretary José Blanco and Bono himself, after a meeting with Zapater, which took place today at noon at the party's federal headquarters.
The head of the executive justified the offer to the former minister by saying that in political life "it is important to count on the best" and Bono "is among the best."
He further noted that "yes, it seems" that this politician from La Manchy will become a candidate of the Socialist Party for President of Congress if the Socialists prevail in the next election.
Bono, as president of Congress, would replace Manuel Marína, who on November 15 published his decision not to run in the next general election and abandon his active political career so that he could devote himself to the fight against climate change within university education.
The former minister acknowledged that the prime minister constantly urged him to return to the top policy he left two years ago.
He further stated that just as citizens know who will be deputy economy secretary if the PSOE wins the election, his party wanted "transparent form" to announce who will preside over Congress.
If Bono wins this position, he hopes to transform the House into a "house of respect and not insults or screams" and achieve that "it will become more and more like to citizens."
“Sadly, unfortunately, it seems that the only place in Madrid, where it is not possible to speak in peace is Congress, they have interrupted the Prime Minister in the Senate on one occasion 26 times.”
Likewise, he uttered commendative words about the current chairman, Manuel Marín, and "his good work," and he didn't want to imagine himself in his place yet, so as not to diminish how he stated what his adversaries might consider "greater."
Climatic Propaganda
Giving the anti-climate change movement subjects ruthless persecution both to those who are sceptical of its predictions and by those who prefer other political and social struggles, it must demonstrate strong coherence.
Moreover, to have the leader of the movement, who is prime minister like Zapatero and believes that this fight is more important than the fight against Islamic terrorism and even intends to sign a "new treaty with the planet."
This proposal forces him to explain why the activities or inactions of his government have made a significant contribution to Spain's non-compliance with the Kyoto Protocol.
Who is responsible for this non-fulfillment, in which sectors this is happening and why its government, the government of the treaty, has not prevented it.
Moreover, it forces him to explain as clearly as possible to the Spaniards what measures he intends to take in order to hinder climate change.
Which companies will be harmed by this, which habits of the population will have to be changed, what energy savings, individual and collective, we will have to accept and what the economic consequences will be.
In other words, who sacrifices himself and who pays to save the planet.
But we didn't get such explanations, we won't even wait.
Precisely because you have to sacrifice and pay.
Measures against climatic changes, genuine measures, are unpopular.
Expensive, inconvenient and threatening corporate and private accounts.
Even one cent of the price of gasoline, the proposed PSOE (Spanish Workers' Socialist Party), seemed so risky to Zapatero in terms of elections that he hurried to reject it.
In other words, it's about saving the planet in such a way that it doesn't cost anything, pure propaganda, slogans lacking any real meaning.
Impressive phrases, commissions, committees, protocols and new posts.
And by covenants with the planet; this master so useful for election campaigns, who does not speak, accuses, demands nothing, but is an adornment of meetings and provokes tenderness.
If it confines itself to being just a virtual party and doesn't ask us to ride a bike and cool down with a fan.
The mayor and head of Totany's local police detained on suspicion of corruption practices during construction
