20 000 people participated in the first Spice Girls tour in Vancouver
Spice Girls started their first tour in Vancouver (Canada) before 20,000 spectators since they broke up in 2000.
"Posh Spice" (Victoria Beckham), "Scary Spice" (Melanie Brown), "Baby Spice" (Emma Bunton), "Sports Spice" (Melanie Chisholm) and "Ginger Spice" (Geri Halliwell) shortly before starting their concert at GM Place stadium in Vancouver declared that their group's reunion was "great."
About 20 000 people bought tickets so that they could be present at the concert, which also included a video that depicts the history of this group, which sparked a sensation in the late 1990s.
The return and world tours take place at the same time when a new album is being released by EMI in Spain, and which brings a selection of their main successful songs, as well as two new songs, "Headlines" and "Voodoo".
Five Spice Girls have appeared on stage adorned with bracelets donated to them by their husband "Posh Spice," British footballer David Beckham.
This athlete was in an audience that flooded GM Place, even though nervous Victoria banned him a few days ago.
Prior to the start of the show, Victoria Beckham stated about the British star that "he didn't see our costumes at all, so I'm nervous about what they think about the show."
"Sporty Spice", whose departure from the group in 1998 "due to contradictions between us" caused the dissolution of a five-member group two years later, she also signaled her nervousness during a meeting with reporters two hours before the show began.
“I was scared.
I was nervous, but I saw that world public opinion had changed, the opinion of the Spice Girls had changed.
And I thought, why not, so I'm glad I decided," Chisholm said of reuniting the group she dissolved nine years ago.
The singer also noted that over time "things have settled and we all love each other very much."
However, a lot has changed since Spice Girls last performed together in 1998.
In the beginning, according to CTV, the group flew to Vancouver with a Virgin Airlines special.
On their arrival at the airport, an army of nannies awaited them.
Five have a total of 7 children.
After a concert in Vancouver, the band will perform in California (USA), San Jose (December 4) and Los Angeles (December 5 and 7).
He will perform in Madrid on December 23.
Worries about a new gas quarrel between Russia and Ukraine
There is currently a dispute between Russia and Ukraine over natural gas prices, thus increasing the risk that Russian supplies of this fuel to Western Europe may be affected.
Most of Russia's fuel exported to the European Union (EU) is routed by a pipeline through Ukraine, and any dispute between the two countries is closely monitored.
Kiev has warned that if Moscow raises gas prices for Ukraine, it will charge Russia higher transit fees.
The previous dispute between the two last year reduced supplies to EU states.
The latest dispute between Kiev and Moscow comes as the Ukrainian government and Russia's state-owned gas monopoly, Gazprom, is trying to agree on how much Ukraine will have to pay for Russian gas in 2008.
Gazprom says it will have to charge Ukraine more because it has agreed to pay up to 50% more for the gas it receives from neighboring Turkmenistan.
Analysts say this could mean Ukraine will have to pay 40% more for its supplies from Gazprom.
"It is important for us to use our arguments as one of the most important gas transit states in the course of the negotiations," Ukrainian Economy Minister Anatoly Kinach told Reuters.
"Changes in gas prices should undoubtedly be linked to our arguments regarding the amount of transit charges (to Western Europe) through Ukraine."
The last major dispute between Moscow and Kiev caused a reduction in supplies from Gazprom to Ukraine in January 2006.
This has caused a crushing fall in exports to Western Europe, sharply raising prices.
Russian gas accounts for one-quarter of supplies to the European Union.
Russia has the world's largest gas reserves, but a large proportion of them remain underused.
As a result, Gazprom complements its own gas supplies from Turkmenistan and other former Soviet Central Asian republics.
NHS 'must control foreign employees'
The inspector said the NHS chambers of Scotland had to improve checks on criminal records among non-European employees.
The recommendation from the president of the Supreme Audit Office, Robert Black, came after a foreign doctor operating in Scotland was linked to June's attacks at Glasgow Airport.
Mr Black is also concerned that Scotland's NHS does not have an accurate idea of the amount of foreign staff he employs.
But his report highlighted that other key inspections took place in order on a sample of five health authorities.
The president of the Supreme Audit Office was interested immediately after the attacks on Glasgow Airport immediately after the attacks on Glasclas Airport on personnel recruited from countries outside the European Economic Area.
Twenty-eight-year-old Bilal Abdullah, an Iraqi doctor at Royal Alexandra Hospital in Paisley, was detained at the airport after someone drove Jeep into the door of the main terminal building.
He was one of three doctors who appeared at the Central Criminal Court using a video bridge accused of links to attempted bombings in the UK.
NHS chambers are expected to inspect foreign staff in terms of qualifications, experience and authorization to work in the UK prior to its employment.
Although the Scottish Health Commission uses Disclosure Scotland to check criminal records, that agency has no authority to obtain such information outside Britain.
A report by the president of the Supreme Audit Office says, among other recommendations, that the commissions should be guided by Criminal Records Bureau directives, which helps employers with controls in several countries.
"While we have found that part of the personnel records contained evidence of these inspections, they are not carried out regularly," the report announced.
Mr. Black said Scotland's NHS in many ways coincides with its own pre-employment clearance policy, but also added: "Our report highlights areas in which the system could be improved to ensure the availability of accurate information on the amount of foreign personnel."
BMA chairman Dr. Peter Terry said it was "very unlikely" that pre-employment checks would identify potential NHS employees who could commit an act of terrorism in the future.
"The report suggests that the current pre-employment controls in the Scottish NHS are working well and it is unclear what additional value those comments will bring," he said.
Scotch health authorities have said they currently have 1,161 foreign workers, the vast majority of whom are doctors and nurses.
However, the president of the Supreme Audit Office pointed out that these numbers will probably also be higher because the commissions had trouble identifying personnel with a residence permit.
The survey also found that established control procedures for NHS authorities in Ayrshire and Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire and Lothian are largely exercised.
They included passport checks, work permits and qualifications.
The Scottish Government is currently revisinging policy and pre-employment control guidelines for NHS foreign workers.
Health Minister Nicola Sturgeon said: "I wanted to handle this work immediately after the terrorist attack on Glasgow Airport."
It also added that the Scottish Government, in agreement with health commissions, will consider the recommendations of the report and will ensure that best practices appear in updated pre-employmentary control directives of all personnel - including the foreign one - it plans to extradite next month.
Joy Division dominated film awards
Control, a biographical film about the late Joy Division singer Ian Curtis, has racked up five awards at the British Independent Film Awards, including the Best Picture award.
The awards were also won by star Sam Riley, who worked at the warehouse, director Anton Corbinn and Toby Kebell.
Lady Judi Dench has been named Best Actress for the thriller Notes of the Scandal.
And Bond star Daniel Craig won the Variety award for taking British film to the world stage.
The awards were solemnly handed out at London's Roundhouse.
Control describes Curtis' journey to fame with Joy Division until his suicide in 1980 at the age of 23.
This black-and-white film - whose soundtrack includes The Killers, David Bowie and the New Order - cost just 3 million pounds.
But he won the Dutch photographer Corbijn, formerly known thanks to the iconic coverage of Joshua Tree's U2 album, two awards - for best directing and for best debuting director.
Rock music was the focus of another awardenee - as Julien Temple's film about Clash singer Joe Strummer won the award for best documentary.
Viggo Mortensen has been awarded the badge for Best Actor for his role as the driver of a Russian mafia boss in David Cronenberg's London thriller Eastern Promises.
Ray Winstone, who has a reputation based on the roles of tough guys, has won the Richard Harris award for his extraordinary contribution to British film.
This fifties, whose films include Scum, Nil By Mouth and Sexy Beast, is currently filming another episode of Indiana Jones - The Kingdom of the Crystal Skull.
Winstone said he was proud of this award, which is named after the star of the films Camelot and Harry Potter Harris, who died in 2002.
"People like Richard Harris and Albert Finney opened the door for people like me," he added.
"I've been lucky enough to meet Richard Harris several times.
He's my man, the great actor of his time, I'm very honored.'
Independent film awards, known as Bify, are awarded by a jury in which filmmakers, actors and organizers of the festival meet.
Proponents of the Bif include Mike Figgis, Ken Loach, Ewan McGregor, Samantha Morton, Bill Nighy, Meera Syal and Michael Winterbottom.
Saudi Arabia arrests 208 people over terrorist conspiracy
Saudi Arabia announced yesterday that it had arrested 208 militants because of cell membership preparing an imminent attack on oil facilities, as well as clerics and security forces.
State television told the country's largest oil exporter that one of the cells wanted to smuggle missiles into the country.
Al-Qaida sympathizers have campaigned against the monarchy, an ally of the United States, since 2003 since the invasion of Iraq.
A televised report reported that a cell of eight militants led by a foreigner was planning an attack on an oil facility in Ash-Sharqiyah province.
Saudi Arabia is building a 35,000-member rapid-responsion unit to defend facilities since the failed al-Qaida attack on Abqaiqu, the world's largest oil-processing plant in 2006.
The report quoted an internal statement from the ministry: 18 of those arrested belonged to a cell led by an "expert to launch missiles" that slipped into the ground.
She went on to say that the militants had planned to smuggle eight missiles into the kingdom.
22 detainees were supposed to prepare the murders of clergy and security personnel.
The government urged clerics to try harder to prevent the Saudis from going to Iraq to join al-Qaida militants fighting allied troops and a U.S.-backed Shia government that conservative Sunni Saudis consider heretical.
Al-Qaida militants believe authorities have co-opted many clerics in Saudi Arabia to support the royal families who control the government.
Militants - backed by al-Qaida leader Sauda's calls Osama bin Laden to target pro-Western Saudi government - have focused on foreign residential complexes, government buildings and energy facilities.
"This war has been a huge effort by security forces in the past...five months," an internal ministry spokesman told state television.
She was also arrested a 16-member "media cell" in Medina, which aimed to promote "Takfirian thinking" - the ideology of Sunni Muslim radicals, which promotes violence against Muslims labeled as unbelievers and renegades.
Among those arrested were also 32 people accused of providing financial support to militants.
The climate on Venus is perhaps a glimpse into the future of the Earth
The researchers announced yesterday that Venus may have once been a pleasant place to live.
Data from the circulating European station shows that the planet is even more similar to Earth's willful twin than previously thought.
Venus is Earth's closest planetary neighbor.
These two worlds are about the same size and circulate along similar tracks, but Venus is a little closer to the Sun.
Scientists now believe that billions of years ago there were conditions similar to those on Earth on Venus, and it could have been habitable.
But the released greenhouse effect caused by carbon dioxide, atmospheric erosion by solar radiation, and a lower rotational rate sent Venus along a completely different path of development.
Today, the planet is without water, the temperature on the surface is hot enough to melt the lead and is devastated by storms and hurricane-force winds.
Findings from the European Space Agency Venus Express, which has been studying the planet for one year, offer a sparing look at how Earth could develop in the event of catastrophic climate change.
Professor Fred Taylor of the University of Oxford's Department of Physics and one of the Heads of the Venus Express mission said: "It's getting clearer and brighter what the climate on Venus is so different than on Earth, even if the planets themselves are quite similar.
"Our new dates make it possible to draw up a scenario in which Venus, like the Earth, perhaps with a habitable environment, began billions of years ago."
Police detain 'encomoured uranium'
A kilo of radioactive material, allegedly enriched with uranium, was detained yesterday by Slovak and Hungarian police.
A police spokesperson said three people had been detained and the material was being investigated.
He did not confirm that it was enriched uranium, but said that the substance could be sold for 1 million dollars (480 000 pounds).
It's still open
Mitt Romney said it wasn't dignified for presidential candidates to answer queries from a snowman.
In spite of this, Republican presidential candidates later agreed to a debate on YouTube, in which ordinary voters recorded their queries on video and handed them over for airing candidates.
None of the questions aired on Wednesday, November 28, were as comical as the snowman who asked Democrats about global warming in the other-party debate on YouTube.
But the questions and even the answers this time reflected a lot about the state of the Republican Party towards next year's elections.
It's unclear exactly how CNN's producers, one of the sponsors of the debate, selected clips, which were then shown to aspiring candidates.
But the first half-hour of the debate, the longest time devoted to a single theme, was focused on immigration.
This topic is one of the most powerful Republican topics.
CNN asked a panel of undecided voters to turn the wheel when they heard something gratifying: the announcer recorded the pinnacles of approval when the candidates spoke of a tough approach at the border.
The toughest and longest exchange between Rudy Giuliani, the main nationwide candidate, and Mr. Romney poked ahead in key early votes in Iowa and New Hampshire.
Mr. Giuliani is trying to shake off the reputation of a man who, as mayor of New York, was soft on illegal immigrants.
He attacked Mr. Romney, accusing the former Massachusetts governor of hiring illegal immigrants to work on his home.
These two struggled particularly fiercely when interviewers asked if any of the candidates would support an "amnesty" (giving illegal immigrants normal status and the possibility of citizenship).
Supporters such as Duncan Hunter, a California congressman, and Tom Tancredo, a congressman from Colorado, were able to throw their little bit into the mill on the subject.
None of the two have a chance of being nominated, but both remain in the game perhaps because they've started hitting the alarm because of immigration earlier and harder than the others.
At least on immigration, candidates have roughly the same view: greater rigor at borders, no tolerance for illegal migrants.
This theme, coupled with fears of poisonous toys from China and other worries with the outside world, helps Republicans forget or minimize the contradiction between liberties and Christian conservatives.
But the debate became very uncomfortable when it came to other topics.
Mr Giuliani was asked why he had supported the possession of firearms in the past.
John McCain and Mr Romney were discussing whether simulated heating technique should be considered torture.
One interviewer asked if the candidates believed every word from the Bible.
Mr. Giuliani stumbled through it to the conclusion that even though the Bible is his favorite book, he probably doesn't literally believe the story of Jonah and the whale.
Another interviewer asked if Jesus would support the death penalty.
Mike Huckabee - who is now first against all assumptions in a recent survey in Iowa thanks to his long-term Christian conservatism - said that the death penalty is unpleasant but necessary.
Under the pressure to decide if Jesus would agree, he said Jesus was too smart to run, thereby earning himself a laugh.
The battle remains extremely open.
Mr Huckabee's rise in Iowa coincides with recent achievements in raising money and Ron Paul's loud merries during Wednesday's debate.
These two have bounced back from the bottom, as both wings of the party are still unable to agree on more well-known and traditional candidates.
In 1980 and 2000, one of the candidates united the party very soon and convincingly with a winning report of American-size on its lips, blinking at both wings of the party.
This time, the side is divided and the result is far from unambiguous.
Pain by Labour
Gordon Brown has once again found a trap he could fall into; his way out remains the same.
Although this is customary in British political scandals, this one is not particularly spicy.
It doesn't seem like any orders have been sold, no bills have been mysteriously paid, no extramarital fingers have been sucked.
But barricaded Gordon Brown would do without discovering that some wealthy businessman for four years masked contributions by the ruling Labour Party of 650 000 pounds ($1.3 million) under the names of other people, and that the party didn't announce it.
Two months ago, when the prime minister avoided calling an election and offended British intelligence by claiming that the decision was not influenced by polls, there was hope that he would secure a resilient and effective government.
Instead, as the increasingly funnier Liberal Democrat leader put it, the once-dreaded Mr Brown changed from Stalin to Mr. Bean.
The clumsy bailout of the troubled Northern Rock bank showed weaknesses in proceedings and feeble judgment under fire.
Poorly thought-out proposals to change the tax code have brought companies together against the government.
The loss of personal data in 25 million people revealed unimaginable administrative mischievousness.
Retired three-generals kicked off riots in the House of Lords; they objected to cuts in military spending, claiming that being the commander of the armed forces was part-time work.
Now there are much-hearted donations from David Abrahams; Peter Watt, secretary-general of Labour (and their former internal discipline keeper), has already resigned.
None of this means that Mr. Brown is inevitably moving towards his end.
Despite this, governments can reach a tipping point, beyond which they reunite the heavy.
The people don't trust politicians and don't like them, but most of the time they displace their disbelief when a new bunch takes over.
When it seems clear that some prime minister probably won't improve anything, and he doesn't even have to be in office for a long time, it's a displacement: the state government is becoming punchy, ministers start doing briefings, the press is looking for bad news, and the government is becoming unfocused and defensive.
In one direction, Mr Brown's position is particularly complex, because compared to other political leaders, he cannot take the position of a new broom.
As much as he'd like to distance himself from Tony Blair, he was his first officer.
For example, problems with Northern Rock and financial office records are linked to decisions that Mr Brown made as Treasury Secretary.
And even though he wasn't directly involved in Blairian problems, such as a long investigation into the financing of political parties caused by "ranks for money," voters can calmly conclude that he was.
Still, Mr Brown's difficulties aren't as bad as they look.
Those mistakes aren't as terrible as the Conservatives do.
As far as Mr. Bean is concerned, no one died or been downright deceived.
And the treatment, however painful it may be, is to provide what Mr Brown's Stalinist tendencies have always promised: a reliable, firm and slightly brutal government.
The latest commotion around political donations is a good example.
Garboid Mr Abrahams, who compares his donations to charity, says he didn't want any publicity for his charity.
That may be true, but the decision wasn't up to him.
Laws against the anonymity of large political donors are clear and exist for a simple reason: the public has the right to know who could buy access to those who rule the country.
Mr Watt, who said he knew about Mr. Abrahams' progress but didn't know it was illegal, would certainly be thoroughly interrogated; so will Jon Mendlesohn, Labour's main money-seeker, who learned of tricky gifts in October.
But so far, Mr. Brown is not involved; there is nothing to imply that he knew about these plots, although the main part of the payments took place only after his arrival in June.
So far, the Prime Minister has done half the work.
He very quickly condemned this violation of the laws by his own party, but diluted his determination by reaching for a tried-and-tested instrument, the commission to investigate the matter - led, moreover, by a retired bishop and judge.
In this case, the lessons are not complicated.
If the law has been violated, it is a matter for the police.
In addition, there are also other things that Mr Brown has to do, such as finding the Permanent Chairman of the Independent Committee on Standards on Standards in Public Life, which has had none for six months, and moving forward with a genuine reform of the funding of political parties.
This would require Labour, who are in closes, to co-operate with the Conservatives and perhaps even give up their special financial relationship with the unions - bitter pills for a devoted party man like Mr Brown.
But it should face a few simple facts.
Conservatives now have a decent lead in the polls; he has two years to get the voters back; and the only way to earn victories is uncontestable integrity and high competency.
Card Sharks
Is the crisis influencing the inferioral mortgage crisis of the U.S. credit card market?
When there is blood in the water, the dorsal fins naturally begin to pass excitedly.
Now that the U.S. real estate market is withering, predators have focused on the credit card market.
Goldman Sachs analysts say credit card losses could reach $99bn if other variants of consumer loans were infected from inferior mortgages.
Signs of tension are clear.
There is an increase in the number of cases of inability to repay (with more than 30-day payment delays) and depreciated accounts (accounts that the provider deems unenforceable).
HSBC announced last month that it had lost £1.4 billion in the U.S. consumer credit market - also because of insolvent credit card loans.
However, it is still too early to panic.
There are still very few depreciated accounts and inability to repay.
According to Moody's, the 3.89% insolvency ratio in the third quarter was almost the entire percentage point below average.
Deteriorating rates can be partly explained by technical reasons.
The change in U.S. personal bankruptcy laws of 2005 led to a sharp decline in the number of reported bankruptcies, which make a large proportion of credit card losses; the number of reports (and thus the number of depreciations) would therefore increase regardless of whether the general conditions for borrowers have deteriorated.
The sector also shows a good maturity that shows how much consumers repay monthly from their debts.
And the confidence in credit card securities is firm, despite paralysis in other corners of structured finance.
Dennis Moroney of research firm TowerGroup predicts that their 2007 emissions will be 25% greater than last year.
There are definitely direct pathways for the spread of infection between a crisis of inferior mortgages and a credit card market: when they dry up loans for a fortune in real estate, consumers are more indebted through credit cards.
However, credit card issuers look at cash flow rather than the values of guaranteed items, so falling house prices don’t necessarily mean changing the credibility of borrowers.
They can even be beneficial for loan providers.
The motivation of consumers to repay mortgages decreases when the value of their property falls below the value of the loan; as a result, the debt on cards is shifted up to a list of repayment priorities.
Card issuers are also able to respond more quickly and flexibly to more tumultuous conditions than mortgage providers by changing interest rates or debt limits.
This, in theory, should reduce the risks of a rapid change in the prices of guarantees.
"It doesn't happen that we just re-evaluate all the loans on one beautiful morning," says Gary Perlin, chief financial officer at Capital One.
Although the risk of an explosion in the style of inferior mortgages is low, the risk of gradual weakening is much more real.
If lower house prices and debt reduction push America into a recession, the credit card sector will face a darker future.
Beware of those dorsal fins.
British bankers plead guilty in Enron case
Three British bankers, who were supposed to be tried for their roles in fraud linked to former Enron finance executive Andrew Fastow, changed statements on Wednesday and pleaded guilty.
David Bermingham, Giles Darby and Gary Mulgrew were originally guilty in seven fraud allegations, when, along with Fastow, they were supposed to participate in financial scam in 2000 -- enriching themselves at the expense of their employers -- denied.
They were scheduled to stand trial in January.
However, during the trial before U.S. District Judge Ewing Werlein Jr., everyone pleaded guilty to one fraud.
The case of these three former Greenwich NatWest executives, a unit of Royal Bank of Scotland Group PLC, has taken the lead for two years, which took issue.
In the United States, their case is one of the unclosed parts of an investigation launched after Enron's collapse in 2001.
Accusations made against them in 2002 alerted Mr Fastow that he was the target of a government investigation into Enron's collapse.
An indictment against bankers -- who earned the nickname "NatWest Troika" -- claims they came to Houston in 2000 to plot a scam alongside Fastow and his former chief aide, Michael Kopper.
Greenwich NatWest invested in the partner branch of Enron controlled by Fastow, who was the architect of a lot of Enronic fraud that pushed Enron down the spiral towards bankruptcy.
At the beginning of 2002, the bank valued its stake in this branch by zero, but our three Britons knew it was actually of considerable value.
Under Kopper's control, the company bought the bank's stake in this branch for €1 million.
Incriminated bankers paid Koppers 250 000 dollars for a stake in the company.
According to the indictment, Fastow deceptively informed Enron that the energy company would pay $20 million for its stake in that branch of Greenwich NatWest.
However, the 20 million actually went to the British bankers, Fastow and others.
According to the indictment, the bankers received 7.3 million and Fastow, Kopper and others improved by about £12.3 million.
In January 2004, Fastow pleaded guilty in two cases of conspiracy for his role in Enron's collapse.
The British trio were arrested three months later.
Fastow is currently serving a six-year sentence at Louisiana's federal prison.
Kopper is serving a sentence of three years and one month at a facility in Texarkana.
All three Britons were free on bail of $1 million, ordering them to stay in the United States for an upcoming trial.
Enron, once the country's seventh-largest company, went bankrupt in December 2001 after years of accounting tricks, it was no longer possible to hide billion-dollar debts or report failing speculation as profitable.
The collapse cut thousands of jobs, more than $60bn of market value and more than $2 billion in pension insurance.
Enron founder Kenneth Lay and former chief executive Jeffrey Skilling were sentenced last year for their roles in the downfall of society.
Skilling is serving more than twenty-four-year sentence.
Judgments over Lay for conspiracy, fraud and other allegations have been erased after he died of heart disease last year.
Moscow's non-powerlessness
The price of the Gulfstream G550 aircraft, equipped with a Rolls-Royce engine and with sufficient fuel for a nonstop flight from Moscow to Los Angeles, starts at US$54 million.
For some of Russia's rich, however, these are tiny to return.
"It can carry up to 18 passengers," says Dan Firer, a Gulfstream representative.
"But none of them are interested in so many places because it's for a narrow family or a corporate president."
Mr. Firer sat in the Gulfstream booth last Friday at the Millionaire's exhibition, a four-day orgy of lavish consumerism in a mammoth exhibition center on the capital's periphery.
In addition to Gulfstream, more than 200 other suppliers of luxury have built their stalls, hoping that the newly wealthy Russians would lure everything from mahogany-inlaid yachts to diamond-decorated car partitions, beyond which not even 50 Cent would be embarrassed.
Mr. Firer glanced cautiously at the clearly orange Porsche in the next stand.
"That's a nice car, but they're dimes and dimes against us," he said.
"After the United States, Russia is the second-largest market for private jets."
On the waves of oil, money flocks to the Russian capital just as it did when Dallas had its days.
"We went through the same stage in the 80's when some Americans got tremendously rich and dragged to Europe, where they looked at them as materialists," said Marshall Goldman, a fourth-year listener at Davis Center for Russian and Eurasian studies at Harvard.
Just like the Saudi squabblers of the 70's and the Japanese in the 90's, the Russians are becoming the world's most prominent spenders.
The most exclusive luxury brands are looking for their part in this Klondica.
Elsewhere in Moscow this week, Donatella Versace, Tom Ford and Bernard Arnaulto, chairman of the LVMH Group, has pulled in to exchange ideas on how to sell expensive goods in markets like the Russian one.
(The International Herald Tribune, owned by The New York Times Company.)
According to Forbes magazine's list of the richest people, Russia has 53 billionaires with a total fortune of $282 billion.
In addition, a local main institute in the Center for Economic and Financial Research has issued a report on the development of personal wealth in the former Soviet Union, according to which there are 103,000 "second-class" millionaires in Russia, whose assets in the sum give 670 billion dollars.
(The World Bank reports that 15.8% of Russians lived below the existential minimum in 2005.)
"There are hundreds of millionaires in Moscow -- and that's why we're all here," said Nicole Marais, sales and marketing representative of London jeweller David Morris.
She was at the fair for the first time, together with the Russian distributor of this brand, Soho Jewelry Group.
"All women are impeccably groomed, and men are all for a fortune."
When they strolled through the evening clothes on Sunday evening, twenty-one-year-old Evgenia Yumatova and five-year-old Svetlana Turovtsova described herself and her clients as millionaires.
"I've been looking at those cars and I can't decide between Infiniti, Mercedes and Porsche," said Ms. Yumatova, who covered herself in designer clothes as a veteran of the red carpet next to which Joan Rivers looks like a home-maddown.
The fair seemed to be aimed primarily at fresh rich people, brand-driven Russian buyers, who are willing to sit for several hours in road traffic, so that they can walk around the warehouse, which garishly mixes Cadillacy Escalade, platinum fish, Mövenpick ice cream, tailor-made billiard tables, apartments in Dubai, stuffed Gund.
Members of the simple public could buy tickets for 30 euros (44.57 dollars).
"The Russians are interested in which brand is the most expensive and the best," declared Mr. Gijrath, who patriotically occupied the stand of the Dutch porcelain manufacturer Royal Delft before the reproduction of Rembrandt's painting from a hand-painted blue tile in larger-than-size.
"Of course the Russians are more interested in gilt."
And indeed, local vendors say that Russians with old money (by Russian standards it is 10 to 15 years old) have already moved from flashy logos to little-known brands of limited-edits that would never have been shown at a shopping fair.
Even so, several thousand people, whether millionaires, envoys of millionaires or simple tourists, flooded the trade halls every day.
Some of them were shopping.
The first day of the exhibition, last Thursday, Roman Ershov, the local general manager of King Koil beds, said he had sold two mattress sets of silk, cashmere and cotton, interwoven with gold yarns, after 70 000 dollars.
Mikhail Lyubimov, sales manager of A1 Moto, which equips high-end cars with high-end alarms that his company has selled a clearly orange Porsche for 170 000 dollars to a Russian millionaire.
On Sunday afternoon, Andrei Karapetian, president of L.A. Connection, which adjusts luxury cars on request, said he had sold three bars to cars in the style of interconnected chain bracelets for the Rolls-Royce Phantomy after 55 000 dollars.
He showed the reporter a bicycle of a car designed for the Russian casino owner, who was stripped by Swarovsky crystals so that it looked like roulette.
Price: 22 000 dollars for four such.
"That Russian millionaire who wants ostrich-skinned bikes or a portrait of his cubic zirconium doesn't have to ride those bikes yet," Mr Karapetian said.
"He'll put them in his garage of thirty to forty cars."
Although many of his Russian clients are wealthy, Mr Karapetian said the phrase "Russian millionaire" currently means nothing much exclusive, given the big increase in the prices of local properties.
"If you bought an apartment in Moscow three or four years ago, you're probably already a millionaire, at least on paper, if not in cash," he said.
"There's more than you think."
Those who are not millionaires could at least fake wealth at a booth operated by the Russian firm Alliance Continental, which deals with asset management.
The company erected four red British-style telephone boothes stuffed with a column of fake banknotes worth a million dollars.
Azad Karryev, the company's marketing manager, said his team had printed 500 000 fake banknotes for the Millionaires Fair.
Passers-by stopped to throw around a pile of money, or to be photographed by friends with a mobile phone resting in the midst of spare wealth, or simply stuffed a pocket with a few million.
"You know, in Moscow, anyone can feel like a millionaire," Mr. Karryev said.
The death in New Jersey may have been caused by spoiled surgery
It was the day before Thanksgiving when Roland Livingston looked from the large windows of the local restaurant A little healthier and followed the ambulance, who had come to the white New England house two houses down West Scott Avenue.
Soon, the police patrol's car joined.
And then another one.
"Then I saw the medical examiner's van arrived," Mr. Livingston, the restaurant controller, said.
“At that moment I knew someone had died.”
Authorities have learned that the dead woman was Olga Arroya, 41, the executive assistant director of the Newar police department.
Now investigators are trying to determine if she died because of a procedure carried out by an unlicensed plastic surgeon, whose identity police declined to provide, who worked at house number 212 on West Scott Avenue.
Authorities haven't figured out what caused Miss Arroy's death yet, hoping that toxicology tests, the results of which may not be known for weeks yet, will help solve a mysterious death that sent a wave of sadness to the leadership of the Newark police.
Miss Arroy has been an assistant to Police Director, Garry F. McCarthy, since September 2006, and her voice has been known to many in the ward.
"She was loyal, tenacious and working hard," Mr McCarthy said of Miss Arroy's phone interview Wednesday.
“She was my shield.
It would protect me.”
Mr McCarthy said Miss Arroy was the wife of Jimmy Arroyo, a former Newar police officer who works for the Alcoholic Beverage Control Commission, and that she leaves behind two sons, aged 14 and 11, and stepdaughter.
Eileen Walsh, spokeswoman for U.S. Union District Attorney Theodore J. Romankow, said detectives are trying to determine if Ms. Arroy died from unlicensed surgical surgery.
"It's one option," Ms. Walsh said.
“The investigation is still ongoing.”
Authorities said they arrived at about three o'clock in the afternoon 21.
November and found Ms. Arroy on the sofa.
She was pronounced dead at the scene.
More than a week later, the authorities still said little about their investigation or about the owner of the house, which is supposedly Flor Margarita Castro.
Miss Walsh didn't say if the authorities had interviewed Ms. Castro.
Investigators are trying to determine whether part of the house was used as an office.
No one answered in the center of Ms. Castro's house, with a damaged rose bush in the front garden and a Dalmatian barking at the back
Next to the door that lead into the cellar, there was a blue barrel in an open garage with "Ethyl Vanillin" - a substance used as a substitute for vanilla in food and cosmetics.
There were two mountain bikes on one side.
Mr. McCarthy recalled that when he first arrived in Newark after 25 years in the New York Police Department, Ms. Arroy gave him invaluable help in chewing through the intolerant and harsh political culture of his new city.
"She said, 'You should watch out for this or this,'" he said.
"She's always been here when I've come, and she's always been there.
It was embarrassing.”
Hopes that the Fed will cut interest rates is leading to a strengthening of world stock exchanges
Stock prices on both sides of the Atlantic jumped yesterday after market hopes that the Federal Bank would try to calm the lending conditions further by lowering interest rates have been strengthened by reassuring remarks made by Vice-Chairman of the U.S. central bank.
Stock exchanges have jumped in London, New York and across Europe after Donald Kohn, second in line behind Ben Bernanko, the Fed's chairman, said in responding to continued pressure in the financial markets, executives must be "flexible and pragmatic."
As part of an apparent effort to soothe irritated markets amid signs of reviving credit effort, Mr Kohn said the recent arousals "partially reversed some improvements in the functioning of the markets towards the end of September and in October."
He further stated that if increased instability persists, the possibility that the financial conditions of households and firms will deteriorate will increase further.
Investors regard these statements as a signal that the Fed is prepared to deliver additional fresh levies of capital to U.S. markets, and possibly further interest rate cuts, to stabilise credit conditions.
Those notes also helped reassure Wall Street amid more bad news about the U.S. economy when apartment sales showed record-low numbers in October.
The consequences of financial whirling have been underlined by announcement by investment bank Bear Stearns that it will cut 650 jobs, 4 per cent of its global workforce, as part of cost-reducing compensation for heavy losses caused by an unsuccessful bet on inferior housing loans in America.
Only 20 of these posts are expected to be lifted in London, where the bank employs 1,500 people.
In New York, the Dow Jones industrial index rose by 331 points, or 2.55 percent, and closed at 13 289.40, while the more used S&P 500 rose 2.65 percent.
London's FTSE 100 rose 165.5 points, or 2.7 per cent, and closed at 6 306.2, with British blue-chips rising more than 39 billion pounds.
Germany's Dax index rose 2.6 percent and France's CAC40 rose 2.3 percent.
Some time ago, credit tensions in the markets turned out to be at full strength after the European Central Bank's three-month-old money packages were lent to European commercial banks at a rate of 4.7%, which is the most since April 2001.
The pressure on the English bank to follow the Fed and the ECB and release above-standard money into London's money markets has increased, saying the rates of Britain's three-month interbank "Libor" loans have risened after uninterrupted growth since November 12 at their maximum from the end of September - 6.59 per cent.
England must decide: Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
We've got two dilemmas here, one almost as heavy as the other.
Who'll be number six for England this weekend in Kandy, Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
And, in a broader context, no less relevant than Sri Lanka's first Test, who's supposed to make that decision?
The second question is relevant because David Graveney, the chairman of the selection committee who sticks to this post at best at borrowed time, has Peter Moores, the head coach, and Michael Vaughan, the captain, with decision-making to help, and since the table of Hugh Morris, the English team's chief manager, is beginning to fill with applications for the post of national collector, though not necessarily full-time.
The one who will not be placed in the sixth place on the bat will be unhappy, but life is often not fair, and the sport at all.
It's not easy to find what one gets at the top of his profession, while someone else never gets through a lower level, sometimes because of a lack of luck rather than ability.
Not only in cricket, selection is challenging and requires an assessment that, in the end, always has to be subjective.
It's the right time for Bopara to move up?
It's fair to Shah, if so?
We might ask the same in another area, let's say here: John Edwards or "Tubby" Banerjee, both clever young executives with good education and an overview in the field, should be promoted to the commission - the place is only for one.
Who's supposed to say which level is right for crickers if he doesn't have a chance to drop or float to the next one?
Naturally, numbers have a big impact on decision-making, but that's what opportunities have.
In the team, the cricker has to capture the captain's gaze at the right time to see if he's supposed to take a wicket, and the batter needs regular chances in starting positions if he's ever supposed to impress as a one-day cricker.
Catching the view of the collector is more complicated.
Last season, for example, there were many of the best that James Anderson, whose place in the first Test seems certain, even if Stephen Harmison declared himself healthy and pitched as an artilleryman.
Anderson took 32 wickets in first grade at 33 runs on each.
Chis Tremlett took only 29 at an equally modest average, but they both had their moments at their highest level and are hoping for the next.
Whether he'll play at Asgiriya Shah stadium or Bopara could just be a matter of opinion.
Choosing can start or reach the careers of both, or it may not have a big impact.
Four years ago in Sri Lance, Gareth Batty played in every Test and served good performances, five wickets and 40 holiday runs in first class, but it was Paul Collingwood who at the same time struggled for his place, who eventually became a stable Test Cricketer, who, despite having scored twice a hundred points in Australia against Australia, may still be underestimated.
Graveney, Vaughan and Moores are more likely, after one further discussion following today's workout at Asirgiya stadium, will follow their conservative instincts and prefer Shah's greater experience to Bopar's youthful exuberance.
There are arguments for both of them.
For Shah, his sparkling and valuable debut Test Shifts speak at 88 points in Mumbai and 32 hundred in first class compared to the six Boparos, who, at twenty-two, is seven years younger and has excellent statistics against spin pitches.
However, Shah made just ten run-overs in two immediums in May against the Caribique at the Lord's Test, his home pitch at Middlesex, and that, along with the immediate rejection of the pickers when Vaughan wanted to occupy him again for the next game, his firing seems to have taken away some of the vigor that was so evident in Mumbai.
On the other hand, the Essexian universal has joie de vivre in his bones.
There's nothing scaring him on the cricket pitch, and his off-field behaviour symbolizes a recently overturned four-wheel-driving car on the exhibition day of the England team's car sponsor, Volkswagen.
He escaped with slight injuries and probably a shock, but the first thing he pulled out his Vodafone (also sponsored, of course) and called Alastair Cook, his teammate, who was watching it from a distance to ask him excitedly, "Have you seen it?"
Bopara is definitely better in the field, his medium-fast shear pitches on the local playgrounds, which seem to suit him, he distributes effectively, and launches confidently and skilfully.
Choosing the right person is one thing, choosing the right time is different.
Maybe it's the real art of choice.
Personally, I'm inclined to get Bopar his chance now, so I don't want to say that Shah won't excel, if, as Ceylon's teas suggest, it'll be him who's going to play here next weekend.
Buses bring refugees back to work - and to fight against al-Qaida
The first buses full of Iraqi refugees from Syria began arriving in Baghdad yesterday, emptying refugees who had run out of money or had expired their visas, and others who had hoped that the city would return to normal life.
Some even came back to fight the militants who drove them out of their homes.
Mansourian bus station was already outside Damascus by 800 people in an official government convoy about a dozen buses, full of refugees from Syria and Jordania who returned by private transport.
"I've decided to go to Syria after many doctors were killed in Baghdad," Sabah al-Quabaisi, a Sunni doctor, said.
"Today I came back because of what I've seen on TV and what I've been reading about in the paper, because of what's been going on in Baghdad and how it's safe now.
"They say Mahdi's army has stopped killing people," he said, referring to the Shiite militia militant cleric Muktada al-Sadra, who has temporarily ordered his people to stop fighting.
Qasim al-Atwani, a thirty-eight-year-old Shiite teacher from the predominantly Sunni region of Amariyah, returned with his two children because he could no longer renew his Syrian visas and because the local regime did not allow the Iraqis to work.
His Shiite neighbors in Amariya called him, saying a local militia with the support of the U.S. military had helped push al-Qaida out of its positions in the west of the capital, urging him to come back.
One of the new arrivals from Jordan is the leader of a Sunni and Shiite tribe who has been banished by a grenade attack from his home in Sleikh in northern Baghdad after urging his fellow citizens to rise up against al-Qaida.
He came back to fight.
"The day before yesterday, several friends called me and told me that he was launching an al-Qaida militia in Sleikh and that he needed me and the support of my boys.
That's why I came back today," Sabaaah Alu Obeidi said.
Many have talked about the attraction of calm in the capital after strengthening the U.S. military presence and the growth of local "groups of biased citizens" who, with a gun in hand alongside the U.S. military, defend their neighborhood.
But there has been more talk that they have been prompted to return by visa obstructions and the loss of money in Damascus, where more than 1.3 million Iraqis have fled in recent years.
Salaam al-Samarraj, a fifty-one-year-old father of three, voiced suspicions shared among refugees that Nūri al-Maliki, Shiite's prime minister, had asked the Syrian government to impose restrictions on refugee visas, prompting them to return.
More than two-and-a-half million people have fled Iraqi sectarian killers, according to available data.
"Everything was fine before al-Maliki visited Syria and pressured the government to stop giving Iraqis visas so they would have to go back to Iraq," the Sunni pensioner said.
"I can't trust a government that supports the criminals who burned my house after I left."
The partial reason for weakening violence is the fact that Sunnis have been forced out of Shiite areas and vice versa, which in many places reduces tensions.
Fears arise that the sudden influx of refugees will once again stir up sectarian tensions and lead to further combat.
Little Saarland, good education
Germany's smallest Länder plays a leading role in vocational education, excelling even with the international focus of its universities.
Only in one educational area does Saarland worry about the same shortage as the rest of Germany.
"I explained yesterday for the 50th time that we are not French" - this self-ironic slogan appears in Saarland on posters on the occasion of the 50th anniversary of the birthday of this smallest Länder.
At the same time, France and Luxembourg play a major role in the Saar education system, which ranks fifth in monitoring the Cologne Institute for Economic Research (IW).
Universities have traditionally co-operated closely with surrounding countries.
Saarbrücken University was founded by the French in 1948.
To this day, students can finish their studies with a double or even triple diploma.
Which corresponds to a reasonably high proportion of foreign students, which amounts to 15.6%.
After all, an above-average number of Saar children are admitted to college - many of them even without studying at grammar school.
“There is a vocational education system used in Saarland,” says Axel Plünnecke, IW’s educational expert.
By earning more than one in five juveniles, Saarland is more than 7% above the German average.
Compared to the rest of Germany there are rich opportunities in Saarland to combine practical and academic education.
Possibilities to study two disciplines are, with regard to the population, the second best in Germany.
Despite practically oriented education and rising demand, the number of engineering graduates has declined by a third in recent years.
That's whay, the Saaric Ministry of Education has now commanded colleges to create a promotional campaign for engineering disciplines.
"After Piso's first study, we carried out several reforms in general educational schools," says an education ministry spokesperson.
Therefore, the government introduced comparative tests throughout Germany.
In order for children to start learning as soon as possible, the last year of kindergarten is free - a model adopted by other Länder.
If the results of the pupils improve significantly, future comparative studies will show.
In conducting the Pisa study in 2003, the proportion of at-risk pupils declined from 29 to 20%.
In total, however, Saarland did not improve by as much as other federal states.
Bavarians can count better.
And to Saarland's disappointment, Pisa's studies do not compare the knowledge of foreign languages - otherwise Saarland could score points in this area.
The language of the neighbors is mandatory in grammar schools - even though the inhabitants of Saarland are not French.
