Germans at risk of lower payouts in 2007
According to the forecast, German employees are at risk of reducing real wages this year despite a strong boom.
Experts justified their pessimistic forecast for a number of reasons.
According to Hans Böckler’s Foundation, employed people can realistically expect to receive lower wages in 2007 than they did last year.
"The blame lies with a small increase in salaries, an increase in value added tax and an increase in prices for food and energy," Gustav Horn of the research institute IMK (Institut für Makroökonomie und Konjunkturforschung) told the daily Bild.
New burdens on employees may also await unemployment insurance.
Treasury Minister Peer Steinbrück told Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung that for the new year the planned reduction of unemployment contributions to 3.3% is correct and reasonable in this situation.
However, the Federal Labor Agency would no longer be allowed to fall into such difficulties again, so that it would have to demand loans or contributions from the federal government, the newspaper of this social-democratic politician was quoted as saying.
It was published on Tuesday that a price explosion of food and petroleum products drove inflation to its highest level in thirteen years.
In the annual comparison, prices have risen by three percent overall.
Venus is the earth's hellish twin
Despite its life-threatening atmosphere, Venus Earth is more similar than previously anticipated. Europe's Venus Express space probe on Venus has recorded phenomena that also appear on Earth in winter: lightning in the atmosphere and wind voices at the poles.
For almost three decades, scientists have suspected that it was flashing on Venus.
Now Europe's Venus Express has yielded evidence - it has captured electromagnetic waves on the edge of the atmosphere. On Venus, lightning flies from cloud to cloud at an altitude of about 56 kilometers, Christopher Russel of the University of California of Los Angeles wrote in the scientific journal Nature.
According to a well-known theory of the formation of primordial matter, life may have arisen on Earth due to lightning. Storms have reportedly provoked reactions in the oceans that led to the formation of amino acids, the cornerstones of life.
It wasn't: "If life ever existed there, it must have been a long time ago," explains Sean Solomon of the Carnegie Institute in Washington.
Today, something alive could hardly be found on the surface of Venus. The atmosphere is about a hundred times denser than on Earth, and sometimes there are temperatures around 500 °C.
"Venus may be the earth's infernal twin, but it's a two," Russel said.
Why Venus, which is so similar to the Earth in matter and size, began to evolve in a completely different way, is the fundamental question of the Esa mission.
Due to the fact that no probe has been deployed since the early 1990s solely for the purpose of studying Venus, the launch of the Venus Express spacecraft in November 2005 was a milestone in this planet's research.
According to scientists, the latest results of the research, which came close to several articles of the journal Nature, did not only emphasize the uniqueness of Venus in the solar system.
"They have also shown that our neighboring planet is more similar to Earth than previously anticipated," Esa said.
In addition to the already known windy vortexes at the North Pole, scientists have found a cloud vortex at the South Pole that spins considerably faster.
This phenomenon resembles air currents in the Earth's northern hemisphere, writes a research team led by Guiseppe Piccioni from Italy's Instituto di Astrofisica Spaziale e Fisica Cosmica.
The second innermost planet in our solar system consists of almost the same matter as Earth, and with a diameter of 12 100 km, it is almost as large.
This planet, referred to as Jitřenka or Večernice, orbits the sun in almost 225 days.
Around his own axis, however, it rotates much more slowly. One day on Venus lasts 243 days on Earth.
Although Venus does not resemble a surface temperature of 460 °C and a pressure of 92 bar, scientists are based on the fact that the two planets formed under very similar conditions.
Water accumulated on both, but it remained in the oceans on Earth, evaporating from Venus into space.
While carbon dioxide on Earth is bound in carbonates on Earth, on Venus they make up most of the atmosphere.
Köhler defends Merkel meeting with Dalai Lama
Federal President Köhler is calling for an end to partisan disputes over the Dalai Lama's reception in the chancellor's office.
Indirectly, he criticised the SPD for her attacks on the chancellor after a mutual meeting in September.
Federal President Horst Köhler is worried about Germany's reputation and calls on a grand coalition to put an end to the Welfare surrounding the Dalai Lama's acceptance.
"We shouldn't outwardly present ourselves as a nation that is not uniform on this issue," Köhler told economic newspaper Handelsblatt.
At the same time, he expressed sympathy for German Chancellor Angela Merkel's (CDU)'s decision to accept the Dalai Lama in September.
"While I respect cultural differences, human rights have universal validity for me," Köhler said.
The federal president emphasized that everyone knows that Germany must maintain a sensible balance in foreign policy, but this did not prevent him from defending Merkel when she was reproached for creating a new policy towards China.
"The Federal Chancellor did not abandon China's traditional unified policy.
China knows it too," Köhler said.
