Musharraf reigns for the first time in plainclothes
The day after his resignation as commander-in-chief of the army, the Pakistani ruler Musharraf took a presidential oath.
That's the end of eight years of military rule in Pakistan.
The president of Pakistan, Parviz Musharraf, took a presidential oath on Thursday and will remain in the presidency for another five years.
Pakistani television broadcast a live ceremony held at the presidential palace in Islamabad's capital.
Musharraf vowed to honor the constitution and strive for the welfare of the nation.
On Wednesday, he handed over his office as commander-in-chief of the military to his confidante Ashfaq Kiyani, subjecting himself to international pressure, especially from the U.S.
Eight years after seizing power in a bloodless military coup, the former general rules for the first time as a civilian head of state.
By withdrawing from the position of commander-in-chief of the armed forces, Musharraf fulfilled one of the main demands of the opposition and the West.
Another central requirement is the abolition of the state of emergency that Musharraf declared on November 3.
According to Malika's chief prosecutor Muhammad Qayyum, Musharraf will lift the state of emergency soon after its oath, thus complying with another of the West's demands.
The country's highest court, which Musharraf, after declaring a state of emergency in early November by his inclined lawyers, dismissed all objections in the past week about the disputed re-election of the head of state on October 6.
Meanwhile, five soldiers were killed in an attack on a military convoy in the North Waziristan tribal region, and four others were wounded.
An army spokesman said the bomb exploded on a road near the border with Afghanistan.
Krautrock's fathers are coming back
The famous German band of the 1970s is playing again. Three members of the Harmonia group took up their electronic synthesizers again at a concert in Berlin.
Revival will also include Live 1974, a haircut from the band's most successful period.
They were well-known abroad at that time.
The announced sensation then consists in the performance of three settled gentlemen, who at first look as if they were just enjoying dinner.
Everyone has boxes, cables and especially buttons in front of them.
Sixty-three-year-old Dieter Moebius is sitting in a snow-white shirt in the middle.
Michael Rother, who has a laptop and a guitar in front of him, is on his left.
Seventy-three-year-old Hans-Joachim Roedelius, who bends over synthesizers intently, is from the point of view of the audience on the right.
Everyone contributes sounds, sometimes even beats.
Sometimes, certainly by accident, a traditional composition will be created.
It doesn't seem to bother the musicians very much.
Among krautrock lovers, Harmonia's revival has caused a considerable stir.
One thing worth noticing. Repeated questions have shown that this name is part of the collective awareness of pop, but it is certainly not common enough for someone twenty-three years to miss this band painfully.
But why couldn't even a German celebrate his ever-living legends from the 1970s?
And especially when he values the musical style, mistakenly called krautrock, in England and America more than in his own country.
In 2003, when the music business was awarded to the surviving members of the Kolín band Can the Echo Award for their life’s work, the hall was full of the questioning glances of German pop fans.
Moreover, when the Red Hot Chili Peppers thanked La Düsseldorf and Neu! during their speech, the audience’s amazement no longer knew the boundaries.
Michael Rother later sat with them on stage like an old friend.
The problem of ignorance at home and adoration abroad is one thing.
By remembering the band Harmonia unnecessarily overwhelming the memory, this trio existed for only two years, and even at that time it was far from having such an influence as others later attributed.
Moebius and Roedelius had previously starred with artist Conrado Schnitzler under the promising name Cluster.
Initially, they played in the cellar of Berlin's Schaubühne Theatre.
Then in Düsseldorf, for economic reasons at a gramophone company led by the church.
Rother briefly assisted with Kraftwerk, but he gained a reputation as a guitarist with the band Neu!, which at that time may have been the most radical German band.
In 1973, Rother followed Cluster's musicians into exile at Forst in Weserbergland.
There, in Old Weserhof, a 16th-century ruin, they lived as members of a commune who fled the city.
They didn't live here as ideologically as in Commune I, where Roedelius was still allowed to toggle like a nanny.
It was more of a life in such Spitzweg artistic being.
The name for the art is supposedly derived from the name of Harmonia Ottenstein, whose badge was supposedly found in a dumpster.
Much of this has been preserved by Harmonius.
The first album was "Music by Harmony" and "De Luxe".
Although their music sounded so calm and modest, the band suffered from financial difficulties.
Collecting rasks didn't just serve self-knowledge.
The now released "Live 1974" is said to have been edited in front of a record-breaking small, barely fifty-member backdrop.
At that time, Rothera expressed his wish that the members of Harmony would be trained and practiced occasionally.
For Roedlio and Moebia, this was an absolutely indecent requirement.
In 1975 the band announced their breakup.
Rother ended up in the esoteric sphere and secured his future with the album "Burning Heart."
Cluster has come to life again in the 1980s and has been more successful ever since.
We can be grateful to Herbert Grönemeyer for the live album from Griessemer.
His recording firm Grönland has taken the goal of cherishing krautrock as an original German contribution to live music.
At that time Roedelio's solo work was considered the band Neu!.
The Physical Resurrection of Harmony will take place in the House of World Cultures in Berlin.
Performances at the Woldtronics festival will be publicly sponsored.
Present will be British, Julian Cope, old punk and the author of "Krautrock Sampler".
The founder Love Parade, Dr. Motte, will also participate.
In doing so, we outlined the overall of Krautrock's influence on future pop music.
The empty place of the famous group will be filled by Harmonia.
Brian Ene's theorem, accompanied by the strangest noises, floats in the hall: "Uniquous true music, it belongs to the future."
There are paintings in which a producer with Harmony is in a peasant sednice.
The creation of the legend is also contributed by the projection of other black-and-white photos, on which there is a set of devices wildly connected cables.
Previously, the three veterans are once again preoccupied with improvisation.
No longer by gluing microphones to the organ and the drums, so that they can extract from the instruments those previously unheard self-made tones.
Today, electronic music looks like electronic music.
Three into the technique of immersion sticklers.
The choir of the House of World Cultures does not change that either.
Under the direction of Barbara Morgenstern, she whispers under the sound clouds and in one beautiful place, "Aaaa aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!"
Krautrock has always been a very English term.
It was never rock.
We'd find the roots rather than in the blues at music colleges (Can), in technology (Kraftwerk) or in fleeing artistic and social conventions (Cluster).
He who shouts "'68!" in this place is not so far from the truth, but Harmonia does not allow himself to be disturbed even now.
Even the most obvious is carefully thought out on the scene.
Where does the music begin and when does it end?
This question either deprives a person of his nerves or indulges him to experience fleeting moments of happiness.
Richard Gere just isn't cynical enough
With the release of The Hunting Party and Mörderischer Frieden, two films about the war in the Balkans appeared.
But neither of them is convincing.
The original film either puts too much emphasis on action and shooting, or they are too touching.
This is not prevented by an actor like Richard Gere.
The good news first: War criminals Karadzić and Mladić are still hiding in the mountains of the Balkans, but it is there, in the gloomy background of the Bosnian War, actor Richard Gere finds himself.
Lately, we have lost sight of this most beautiful man in the 1980s, not only because he has played in many pleasing, non-serious roles in relaxed women’s films, but in conjunction with his press conferences forever on the topic of Buddhism, they take away one’s cravings for the next film with this actor.
And yet Richard Gere is all of a sudden the most powerful argument for watching The Hunting Party - Where the hunter becomes prey (Why do movie titles have to be so long and tangled nowadays?).
Gere gives a magical performance. His war reporter Simon Hunt is basically who Gere could become if fate and the film industry weren’t so merciful: a man who, while having his best years behind him, still retains a certain remarkable allure as a debris-similar monument.
But despite this trump, The Hunting Party, along with the German film Mörderischer Frieden, which also enters cinemas this week, is just a continuation of an endless series of unsuccessful films about the war in the Balkans.
Mörderischer Frieden can do a short trial: This film is about two German soldiers in Kosovo (Adrian Topol, Max Riemelt), who rescue a Serbian girl from a Kosovo sniper, whereupon one of them falls in love with her.
While it is laudable that Serbs aren't portrayed here as the cause of all evil, this television-shaky film turns too early into shooting and action.
From the film will remain something very similar to the series Kobru 11, although without a highway, but for that with the war.
On The Hunting Party it is possible to explore clearly why a really good film about the Balkan War has not yet been made.
The story of three U.S. war reporters which set out to be a "fox," Serbia's top war criminal, could perhaps become a new "Apocalypse" at another time and under the direction of another director and screenwriter (here's Richard Shepard).
After all, both stories are surprisingly similar: small groups go into the woods to do something that the army is not capable of, but their path will become a path to madness.
One of the reasons for The Hunting Party's failure is that Simon Hunt isn't that debauched and sentimental cynic presented at the beginning.
On the contrary, the draft will prescribe him a romantic affair with a later murdered Bosnian Muslim woman.
We can definitively write off The Hunting Party after reporters in the last five accelerated minutes of the film do what NATO, the CIA and all the hunters of criminals of the world have failed to do in the last five years: to catch criminals and leave him with the righteous revenge of Bosnian Muslims.
This is so contrary to the logic of filmmaking that it would be worth turning to the International Court in The Hague.
This film has a deeply human message.
He condemns the fact that Karadzić and Mladić are still at large, and he suspects Americans and the French (here they are exceptionally united) that they do not pursue persecution quite intensively on the basis of secret political agreements.
But good and evil are sometimes so similar, women come to think of Brecht's famous poem.
In it is a wooden mask of an Asian demon on the writer's desk: "With sympathy I look at the swollen veins on my forehead, noting: how strenuous it is to be evil.".
In films such as The Hunting Party, they feature exactly the kind of swollen heads that indicate how strenuous it is to be good.
This problem is not limited to the issue of war in the Balkans.
It has recently been reported in these newspapers that all Hollywood films about Iraq have been great failures.
There's only one thing to add to that: there's never been a good film about South Africa.
Almost always true: When a star wanders around a crisis area or walks on a minefield, a film like Rangoon with Patricia Arquette, The Breaking Border with Angelina Jolie or Blutdiamanten with Leonardo DiCaprio is made.
Maybe all these conflicts are just too close in time.
After all, decades had to go by before Hollywood could make a good movie about the Holocaust - Schindler's List.
Many of the films must apparently ripen - films, which today are considered classical Vietnamese works - "Deer Hunter", "Apocalypse" or "Lead Vest" - were by no means hailed as masterpieces.
But perhaps today, when every mass murder at home immediately appears on two screens, we know too much about the conflicts of this world.
Screenwriters then do not have the ability that is needed for flawless storytelling.
"To be or not to be" or "Great Dictator" became so good, because Lubitsch and Chaplin didn't yet know how terrible the Nazi regime actually ruled.
Maybe you can't want to be good when you want to make good movies.
Perhaps he has to form a sarcastically measured artistic view as the novelist Lawrence Norfolk.
Speaking about the parallel between his historical novel "Lemprière's Dictionary" and "Balcanese War," he declared: "The form lies only in the fact that some things never change.
Cities will always be very dangerous, people will always be murdered in a terrible way, and there will always be war in Yugoslavia.”
Von Beust reaps criticism for renouncing the Olympics
The mayor's resignation since hosting the Summer Olympics in Hamburg has come across fierce criticism of politics and the economy.
Many want to continue to fight to organize games by the water.
Von Beust, however, urges realism in an estimation of chances.
Hans-Jörg Schmidt-Trenz, the Chamber of Commerce's chief executive in Hamburg on Wednesday called the decision "unfortunate in terms of business."
No other project has awoken as much motivation in the city as the Olympic idea.
"Without this goal, it would not have been possible to maintain this sporting-political enthusiasm at such a high rate."
Schmidt-Trenz urged the Senate not to bury the project, but created a new long-term concept for it, "which stems from a changed timetable."
Mayor Ole von Beust remained with his earlier statements on Wednesday, according to which Hamburg's Olympic ambitions are unrealistic until at least 2028.
"Not Hamburk, but the German Olympic Sports Association will decide on the Olympic candidacy," von Beust told the WELT ONLINE newspaper.
He pointed out that at the beginning of December, the Sports Association will decide on Munich's candidacy for the 2018 Winter Games.
"This preference means for us that we have no realistic chance with a 2028 schedule."
Still, this dream isn't over yet, von Beust said.
The Hamburg Sports Association (HSB) called von Beust's remarks regrettable.
It gives the impression that Hamburg does not want to compete for the Olympic Games in the next twenty-five years.
"It's still too early to destroy our chances by calling the plans already buried at this early stage," HSB president Günter Ploss said on Wednesday.
Renouncing the application would be contrary to the testimony of the head of the Senate, which he uttered at the general meeting of the General German College Sports Association.
Von Beust did not announce his candidacy of Hamburg for the Universiade in 2015.
In the past week, the Hamburk University Sports Association has only nominated for a candidate city.
The SPD faction in Hamburg's Senate parliament said it was wasting valuable time that could be used to build apartments in Hamburg.
"The development of the Klein Grasbrook area has been hampered by the Senate for years because it bet on hosting the Olympics and ruled out the construction of apartments," SPD deputies Carola Veit and Jan Quast said.
While the SPD for the Klein Grasbroow region was still planning the Olympics, saying that the entire area would later be used for housing purposes, the CDU unanimously bet on an "Olympic factor."
Taking care of the 2012 Summer Olympics sparked great enthusiasm in Hamburg.
However, Hamburk crashed in Leipzig.
They're not Warhol boxes
The one who connects the expressions of Sweden and the wooden crates does not think about Andy Warhol, but on Ikea.
Or yes?
Wooden crates currently produced in Sweden cast dark shadows on Warhol's list.
Most of him on the list of ninety-four "Brillo boxes" like Stockholm are falsifiers.
After Sweden's "Expressen" published doubts a few months ago about the authenticity of Warhol's "Brillo boxes" of 1968, internal research of the Moderna Museet Museum in Stockholm comes with a result that brought disillusionment; the six "Brillo boxes" donated to the Moderna Museet Museum in 1995 by its former director Pontus Hultén are copies - as well as most of the other "Brillo boxes" of the international market.
The previous events are a tangled, confusing chain of artistic and curatorial events.
Warhol made his first Brillo boxes in New York in 1964, painting wooden crates that were based on the red-and-blue-and-white design of Brillo soap powder boxes.
An exhibition of his works at Moderna Museet in 1968 demonstrated - with Warhol's approval - hundreds of Brillo crates of cardboard. These didn't come (for financial reasons) from Warhol, but were delivered directly from the Brillo factory.
It wasn't about works of art, but about their originals, mere exhibition pieces.
Warhol apparently also gave permission in 1968 to produce several wooden Brillo boxes in Sweden, but they aren't exhibited at Moderna Museet.
At that time, witnesses now speak of fifteen crates, which at that time rightly entered artistic and auction history as authorized originals - "Andy Warhol's Brillo Boxes" with the designation Type Stockholm.
Some of them were auctioned for more than 100 000 dollars at auctions.
The problem now is that the legendary director of the Pontus Hultén Museum later falsely claimed that approximately one hundred crates were produced and exhibited in Sweden in 1968, which Warhol then left to him.
In fact, 105 Hultén boxes were produced in Malmö for an exhibition in St. Petersburg only in 1990.
By that time, Warhol had been dead for three years.
Lars Byström, chief conservator of Moderna Museet, explored, in addition to six Brillo boxes owned by the museum, three Brillo boxes from the Swedish private collection, two of which were made in Sweden in 1968, and after their plywood boards were painted with basic paint and sanded, they were painted with an oil white.
On the contrary, it turned out from the structure of copies from 1990 that the paint had been applied directly to the plywood plate without a previous primer - in addition, it is acrylic and not oil paint.
"These crates have not been authorized by the artist and should be removed from Warhol's official Brillo box list," said Lars Nittve, director of Moderna Museet: "If Andy Warhol Art Authentication Board in New York City draws no other conclusions, Moderna Museet will have to classify six crates from its exhibition collections as "exhibit material - copies."
As early as July, the Authentication Board announced that it would address these "mainous allegations," which have been filed on soap-powded boxes.
However, the organization still remains relentless silence.
Rebelling soldiers in Manila are giving up
In the Philippines, rebellious troops surrendered who fortified themselves at a hotel in Manila and demanded the resignation of President Gloria Macapagal Arroy.
Insurgent commander Antonio Trillanes, who accused the president of manipulation and corruption in the election, told reporters at the hotel that he denied responsibility for the bloodshed.
Troops loyal to the government had previously attacked a hotel with the deployment of tear gas.
Just hours earlier, about two dozen soldiers had fortified themselves in the building.
On Thursday, about half of them stood trial because of a thwarted coup attempt in 2003, among them were Trillanes.
He used the negotiations to escape with about 30 sympathizers.
The military officers, who were supposed to guard him, apparently defected to his side and joined the riot.
They broke into the Peninsula hotel and demanded the resignation of President Gloria Macapagal Arroy.
Loyal troops closed the area around the selected hotel.
The president convened an urgent session of the security cabinet.
National security adviser Norberto Gonzales condemned the actions of the soldiers.
"People have had enough," Triallnes said.
"We call on people to join us."
General Danilo Lim, commander of the former elite Scout Rangers unit, said other military units would join the march in a short time.
Critics from the government have called for large demonstrations to take place against the president on Friday.
Corruption has been blamed on her government for months.
Manila's financial area has already become the main scene of a revolt of about 200 soldiers against Arroy in 2003.
Because the army refused to join the insurgents, the riot collapsed after less than 24 hours.
Since 1986, they have tried at least seven times to overthrow a democratically elected government in the Philippines.
United Internet Becomes Versatel's Majority Shareholder
United Internet secured more than a twenty percent stake in Versatel’s telecommunications company.
The company announced on Wednesday that the average price per share was 14.46 euros.
The company now owns 20.05% of its share capital.
Previously, there was speculation of a takeover.
The company is still counting on the possibility of a further increase in its stake in Versatel.
United Internet has announced that it is trying to accelerate the consolidation of the German DSL market.
After Berenberg raised its stake in this Düsseldorf network operator by a further 5% to 15.1%, there were rumours of entry on Wednesday.
In the financial market, it is said that this money institution holds shares only for a third party.
Yesterday, United Internet had almost a 10% stake in Drillisch, a provider of mobile telecommunications networks.
As part of raising capital, Drillisch gave an additional 17.4 million new shares worth 106.4 million euros for sale.
Of these, 5.15 million shares were bought for 31.4 millione by the United Internet.
The companies discussed marketing collaborations in DSL products and mobile networks.
"We're strengthening our partnership," a United Internet spokesperson explained.
In order to take over Freenet's rival company, the companies together established a joint venture of SMEs.
United the Internet announced on Thursday that the two companies in Freenet will settle for a stake of more than 10 percent for the time being and will not exercise their right to take over another 18.49% of the business.
It is considered that so far companies are retaining all options, especially the possibility to further increase their stake in Freenet AG.
Talks between United Internet and Freenet regarding the strategic partnership were concluded in mid-November.
This months-long and hard-to-transparent game about Freenet is part of the consolidation of the German telecommunications market.
At the end of the third quarter, Freenet had 2.9 million contractual customers of the mobile network and 1.27 million customers with complete or DSL connections.
So far in Freenet, United Internet and Drillische held a 6.01% stake in Freenet.
It has now been agreed that Drillisch will receive approximately another 4%.
The right to buy 18.49% of the investment company Vatas was agreed as early as August.
